window.AOS_SEARCH = [{"u":"architecture.html#agent-os--architecture--implementation-reference","p":"Architecture","h":"Agent OS — Architecture & Implementation Reference","x":"Working name only: Agent OS is a provisional development name. The final project/product name will probably change. Do not treat this as a finalized brand, domain, executable namespace, or trademark. Document status: Draft architecture / implementation reference (v0.1) Last reviewed: 2026-11-08 Audience: Coding agents and contributors implementing the distribution Implementation status: Proposed design; the components described below are not assumed to exist."},{"u":"architecture.html#0-instructions-for-coding-agents","p":"Architecture","h":"0. Instructions for Coding Agents","x":"This document is the source of architectural intent, not a claim of implemented functionality. Preserve the native-first approach. Do not introduce a mandatory container, VM, Kubernetes, or custom agent orchestration runtime. Preserve the privilege boundary between the system-maintenance agent and project-scoped agents. Never give ordinary project agents general-purpose sudo access or a privileged host socket. Prefer existing kernel, systemd, and package-manager functionality over custom daemons. A coding harness (Pi, Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, etc.) must be replaceable. Do not bind the OS to a specific harness, model, or provider. Project agents may use normal Linux tools and install project-scoped dependencies. The system agent may request system-wide changes through constrained, auditable authorization. Keep the host headless by default; graphical environments are created on demand per project. Distinguish requirements, proposals, experiments, and open decisions. Do not silently convert an open decision into a fixed design. Implement in small, testable vertical slices. Require a threat-model review for changes involving privilege escalation, credentials, filesystem mounts, networking, or GUI capture. Record architectural changes in the decision log and revise acceptance criteria accordingly."},{"u":"architecture.html#1-vision","p":"Architecture","h":"1. Vision","x":"Agent OS is a headless, remotely accessible Linux development workstation optimized for autonomous coding agents. The operating system can itself be maintained by a coding agent, while each project receives a native development environment with independent identity, state, limits, and permissions. Core principle: The host is an agent-maintainable workstation; projects are agent-operated native Linux environments that do not automatically inherit host administration privileges. Agent OS is not an AI model, coding harness, workflow orchestrator, general-purpose server platform, or permanently running graphical desktop."},{"u":"architecture.html#11-goals","p":"Architecture","h":"1.1 Goals","x":"Run standard coding harnesses and conventional CLI tools natively. Allow a system agent to maintain global packages, toolchains, services, and OS configuration. Allow project agents to install dependencies, modify repositories, build software, and run tests within their assigned scope. Support multiple independent projects and agents on one machine. Share versioned development toolchains without forcing identical versions on every project. Preserve project files, agent state, caches, and persistent sessions. Provision on-demand graphical environments for testing native desktop applications. Capture screenshots, videos, application logs, and optional input-event traces. Support snapshots, recovery, and external backups. Produce installable Debian-derived OS images without building Linux from scratch. Maintain a low idle resource footprint."},{"u":"architecture.html#12-non-goals","p":"Architecture","h":"1.2 Non-goals","x":"For the initial releases: Creating a new Linux kernel or package manager. Replacing APT, Git, systemd, or SSH. Reimplementing agent reasoning, planning, memory, or workflow orchestration. Requiring a graphical desktop, web UI, or specific IDE. Kubernetes or multi-machine scheduling. Mandatory containers or virtual machines. Guaranteeing strong isolation through Unix identities alone. Full process checkpoint/restore. Guaranteed pixel-identical graphical rendering across different hardware."},{"u":"architecture.html#2-architectural-decisions","p":"Architecture","h":"2. Architectural Decisions","x":"Development name Agent OS Temporary Base distribution Debian 13 Stable (Trixie) Preferred Distribution model Minimal Debian + custom packages Proposed Default execution Native Linux processes Requirement Agent roles Human admin, system agent, project agent Requirement Project isolation Dedicated Linux identities, systemd, namespaces Requirement Containers Optional rootless OCI runtime Proposed Virtual machines Optional for high-risk workloads Future System packages APT/dpkg through controlled broker Proposed Toolchains Shared versioned tools + project-local installations Proposed Filesystem Btrfs Preferred Remote access OpenSSH over private networking Proposed Graphics Ephemeral headless Wayland sessions Requirement GUI backend Headless Sway with grim, wf-recorder, and wayvnc. See GUI.md. Decided Legacy GUI XWayland, inside each Sway session Decided Implementation language Rust Preferred Interfaces CLI first, API later Proposed"},{"u":"architecture.html#21-why-debian","p":"Architecture","h":"2.1 Why Debian?","x":"Debian Stable is preferred because the host should remain reliable during long-running autonomous development operations. Modern development tools can be managed independently from Debian packages. This separates: Stable operating system components. Fast-moving programming language toolchains. Project-specific dependencies. AI coding harnesses. Arch Linux remains an alternative if newer system packages and rolling-release development become more valuable than conservative host updates. NixOS remains technically viable but introduces compatibility considerations for arbitrary prebuilt Linux binaries and conventional installation scripts. The distribution choice should be revisited only if benchmarks or implementation requirements justify it."},{"u":"architecture.html#3-trust-and-execution-model","p":"Architecture","h":"3. Trust and Execution Model","x":""},{"u":"architecture.html#31-human-administrator","p":"Architecture","h":"3.1 Human Administrator","x":"The human administrator: Owns the workstation and its security policies. Authorizes high-risk global changes. Manages emergency access and recovery credentials. Can inspect all projects when explicitly acting as host administrator. Defines which system-agent operations may run autonomously."},{"u":"architecture.html#32-system-maintenance-agent","p":"Architecture","h":"3.2 System-Maintenance Agent","x":"The system-maintenance agent: Runs its coding harness under a non-root Linux identity. Can inspect approved host state. Can request changes to packages, toolchains, services, and configuration. Uses a privileged broker for authorized modifications. Has no unrestricted root shell by default. Does not automatically inherit project credentials or write access to project data. Its purpose is to make the OS itself maintainable through agentic coding."},{"u":"architecture.html#33-project-agent","p":"Architecture","h":"3.3 Project Agent","x":"A project agent: Runs as an unprivileged project-specific identity. Can read and modify its assigned project. Can use ordinary Linux executables, compilers, package managers, and development tools. Can install dependencies into project-local directories. Can run native applications, background processes, and GUI sessions. Cannot install global APT packages. Cannot modify global development toolchains. Cannot access unrelated private project files. Cannot invoke privileged host-maintenance operations. A project agent may request that a dependency be promoted to global scope, but cannot authorize that promotion itself."},{"u":"architecture.html#34-security-invariants","p":"Architecture","h":"3.4 Security Invariants","x":"The implementation must test the following: Project A cannot access Project B's private state, credentials, or graphical sockets. Project agents cannot modify host configuration, APT databases, system services, or boot settings. Project agents cannot use privileged sockets or leaked credentials to escalate permissions. The system-maintenance harness itself does not run as root. Global executable directories cannot be modified by project identities. GUI sessions, D-Bus sockets, recordings, and viewer access are isolated by project. Resource limits are not treated as complete security isolation. Kernel vulnerabilities and exposed system services remain part of the threat model. Package installation scripts are recognized as potentially privileged code. Approved system modifications are audited and have tested recovery procedures."},{"u":"architecture.html#35-execution-profiles","p":"Architecture","h":"3.5 Execution Profiles","x":"native-trusted Explicitly trusted interactive development Normal unprivileged Linux process, resource limits native-restricted Default autonomous project execution Project UID, systemd restrictions, namespaces, cgroups container Projects needing stronger dependency or process separation Rootless OCI container vm Highly untrusted workloads or alternative operating systems Separate guest kernel Native-first means the agent uses the host's normal Linux userspace. It does not mean unrestricted filesystem access or administrative privileges. Project isolation should use Linux-native primitives before introducing additional runtimes. Systemd filesystem restrictions have context-dependent limitations, particularly in user-managed units. All relevant restrictions must be verified with negative tests. IPC, D-Bus, host sockets, devices, and network connectivity require separate consideration."},{"u":"architecture.html#4-component-architecture","p":"Architecture","h":"4. Component Architecture","x":"Remote Developer / IDE / Coding Agent | Private Network | +------------------------------------------------+ | Agent OS | | Debian-based, headless Linux host | | | | System-Maintenance Agent | | | | | v | | hostd: Privileged Host Maintenance | | | | workspaced: Native Project Lifecycle | | | | | +----------------------+ | | | | | | v v | | Project A Project B | | Dedicated UID Dedicated UID | | Native Agents Native Agents | | Project Toolchains Project Toolchains | | Project Workspace Project Workspace | | Optional GUI Optional GUI | | | | systemd + cgroups v2 + Linux namespaces | | Btrfs + shared read-only toolchains | | | | Optional OCI / VM runtimes | +------------------------------------------------+"},{"u":"architecture.html#41-workspaced","p":"Architecture","h":"4.1 workspaced","x":"Proposed service responsible for native project lifecycle management. Responsibilities: Create, list, inspect, and delete projects. Allocate and manage project Linux identities. Configure ownership and private project directories. Create project homes, workspaces, caches, artifacts, and state directories. Start ordinary Linux commands and coding harnesses. Configure systemd execution units and cgroup resource limits. Persist project metadata. Manage lifecycle state across disconnections and restarts. Coordinate filesystem snapshots. Authorize project-owned graphical sessions. It should use existing Linux facilities instead of implementing its own process supervisor. A dedicated Rust daemon should only be introduced when shell scripts and systemd units no longer provide a sufficient interface."},{"u":"architecture.html#42-hostd","p":"Architecture","h":"4.2 hostd","x":"Proposed privileged host-maintenance broker. It should: Operate as a narrowly scoped root-owned service. Authenticate requests using local OS identities. Expose a permission-restricted Unix socket. Accept typed operations rather than arbitrary shell strings. Evaluate authorization policies. Record every privileged change. Execute verification and recovery steps. Potential operations: PackageInstall PackageRemove PackageUpgrade ToolchainInstallGlobal ToolchainRemoveGlobal ServiceEnableDisable SystemStatus UpgradePlan ChangeHistory CreateRecoveryPoint RestoreRecoveryPoint The ordinary API must not include unrestricted commands equivalent to: run_as_root(command: string) Such functionality would undermine the agent trust boundaries."},{"u":"architecture.html#43-authorization-levels","p":"Architecture","h":"4.3 Authorization Levels","x":"Automatic Allowlisted low-risk operations with bounded impact, such as selected Debian package installations. Approval required Kernel upgrades, bootloader changes, firewall or SSH modifications, new package repositories, privileged services, and arbitrary global binary installations. Denied Unauthorized project-agent host changes, attempts to bypass policy, and access to unrelated project or host credentials."},{"u":"architecture.html#44-storage-module","p":"Architecture","h":"4.4 Storage Module","x":"Initially implemented as a library rather than an independent service. Responsibilities: Btrfs subvolume creation and deletion. Snapshot creation and restoration. Retention policies. Path validation. Space accounting. Backup integration. Safe coordination with running processes."},{"u":"architecture.html#45-proposed-cli","p":"Architecture","h":"4.5 Proposed CLI","x":"The following commands are illustrative and do not yet exist. agentos project create my-app agentos project shell my-app agentos project run my-app -- pi agentos project snapshot my-app --label before-refactor agentos project restore my-app --snapshot SNAPSHOT_ID agentos system package install ripgrep agentos system change history agentos project gui my-app start demo --size 1920x1080 agentos project gui my-app run demo -- ./target/debug/my-app agentos project gui my-app screenshot demo --output ui.png agentos project gui my-app record start demo agentos project gui my-app record stop demo agentos project gui my-app stop demo The exact CLI and service names are provisional because the project name is not finalized."},{"u":"architecture.html#5-package-and-toolchain-management","p":"Architecture","h":"5. Package and Toolchain Management","x":"Agent OS has three software installation scopes. Host OS /usr, /etc, system directories System agent through broker Shared toolchains /opt/agent-os/toolchains System agent through broker Project tools Project home and workspace Project agent"},{"u":"architecture.html#51-host-packages","p":"Architecture","h":"5.1 Host Packages","x":"APT and dpkg remain the host package management system. The system agent can request installations, upgrades, and removals through hostd. Project agents cannot directly modify the host package database."},{"u":"architecture.html#52-shared-toolchains","p":"Architecture","h":"5.2 Shared Toolchains","x":"Example layout: /opt/agent-os/ toolchains/ node/ 24/ 26/ java/ 21/ 25/ python/ 3.13/ 3.14/ rust/ Global toolchains must be: Versioned. Shared across projects. Readable and executable by authorized projects. Not writable by project agents. Installed through a staged verification process. Selectable independently for each project. Use existing tooling such as mise, rustup, uv, SDKMAN, or vendor-provided installers where appropriate. Do not develop a completely new language package manager."},{"u":"architecture.html#53-project-specific-tools","p":"Architecture","h":"5.3 Project-Specific Tools","x":"Project agents can install and manage tools inside their own scope. Examples: npm install uv sync cargo install --root \"$HOME/.local\" just mise use node@24 Project-local installations should never require global host privileges."},{"u":"architecture.html#54-dependency-promotion","p":"Architecture","h":"5.4 Dependency Promotion","x":"A project agent may identify a tool that would be useful globally. The expected workflow is: Project discovers dependency | v Project requests global installation | v System agent evaluates request | v hostd checks policy | v Install, verify, publish, audit A global tool should become available to projects without exposing the toolchain directory for modification. Privileged executable search paths must never include project-writable directories."},{"u":"architecture.html#6-filesystem-and-persistence","p":"Architecture","h":"6. Filesystem and Persistence","x":""},{"u":"architecture.html#61-preferred-filesystem","p":"Architecture","h":"6.1 Preferred Filesystem","x":"Btrfs is the preferred starting point. Reasons: Copy-on-write snapshots. Subvolumes. Transparent compression. Efficient project cloning. Native Linux kernel integration. Send/receive for replication. STORAGE.md records the quota and retention design and the benchmarks. Compression is still open."},{"u":"architecture.html#62-proposed-layout","p":"Architecture","h":"6.2 Proposed Layout","x":"/ Host operating system /opt/agent-os/ toolchains/ Root-owned shared development tools /var/lib/agent-os/ metadata/ Project definitions and host management state projects/ project-a/ workspace/ home/ cache/ state/ artifacts/ project-b/ workspace/ home/ cache/ state/ artifacts/ snapshots/ Managed recovery snapshots Workspace directories should use explicit subvolumes where lifecycle management requires them. Nested Btrfs subvolumes are not automatically included recursively in parent snapshots. Snapshot boundaries must therefore be planned and tested."},{"u":"architecture.html#63-snapshot-requirements","p":"Architecture","h":"6.3 Snapshot Requirements","x":"Support manual snapshots. Support snapshots at approved task boundaries. Support snapshots before destructive changes. Avoid snapshots after every shell command. Track disk consumption. Apply retention limits. Stop relevant processes before restoring active filesystems. Preserve project metadata necessary to restore workspace state."},{"u":"architecture.html#64-backups","p":"Architecture","h":"6.4 Backups","x":"Snapshots are not backups. Implement an independent external backup mechanism. Potential technologies include Btrfs send/receive and restic. A restore test on a fresh VM must be part of the acceptance suite."},{"u":"architecture.html#65-host-recovery","p":"Architecture","h":"6.5 Host Recovery","x":"Do not assume Btrfs root snapshots automatically restore the bootloader, kernel, initramfs, EFI partition, or independently mounted persistent directories. Host rollback requires an explicitly designed boot and recovery process. For v1, prioritize: Audited change history. Configuration backups. Tested rescue procedures. Known-good installation images. Safe package transaction recovery. Full bootable rollback can follow after proving it works across OS updates."},{"u":"architecture.html#7-on-demand-graphical-testing","p":"Architecture","h":"7. On-Demand Graphical Testing","x":"Agent OS remains headless when idle. Project agents can create temporary graphical environments without installing or launching a permanent host desktop."},{"u":"architecture.html#71-required-capabilities","p":"Architecture","h":"7.1 Required Capabilities","x":"Start and stop multiple named graphical sessions. Associate each session with a project. Configure virtual display resolution. Launch native GUI applications. Support Wayland and, where compatible, XWayland. Provide keyboard and mouse input automation. Capture screenshots. Record videos. Provide optional remote live viewing. Store artifacts and metadata within the project. Clean up orphaned sessions. Work without a physical GPU. Add GPU acceleration later where supported."},{"u":"architecture.html#72-session-modes","p":"Architecture","h":"7.2 Session Modes","x":"Application mode — Default Launch an individual application with a minimal compositor. Targets include GTK, Qt, Tauri, Electron, Slint, browsers, and similar applications. Desktop mode — Optional Launch a lightweight or complete desktop environment for integration testing involving notifications, window management, settings, or multiple applications. Isolated mode — Optional Use an OCI container or VM for applications requiring stronger isolation or a different userspace."},{"u":"architecture.html#73-gui-backend-candidates","p":"Architecture","h":"7.3 GUI Backend Candidates","x":"waymux First experiment. Its published functionality includes headless Wayland sessions, capture, input automation, live viewing, and agent-oriented interfaces. Packaging, reliability, and API stability must be evaluated before making it a dependency. Headless Sway Alternative based on the wlroots ecosystem. Potential companion tools include grim, wf-recorder, wtype, and wayvnc. Weston Alternative compositor with headless and remote backend options. Xvfb Fallback for legacy X11 applications. No backend should become mandatory before testing real applications."},{"u":"architecture.html#74-gui-artifact-layout","p":"Architecture","h":"7.4 GUI Artifact Layout","x":"artifacts/gui/ session-id/ metadata.json screenshots/ 0001.png 0002.png recording.mkv inputs.jsonl logs/ Metadata should describe: Project and session ID. Application command. Display backend. Resolution. Capture timestamps. Recording codec. Process exit status. Test result if available. Input traces may include sensitive information and require redaction and retention controls."},{"u":"architecture.html#75-gui-security","p":"Architecture","h":"7.5 GUI Security","x":"Sessions must run under project identities. Wayland sockets must not be shared across unrelated projects. D-Bus and desktop IPC should be appropriately isolated. Remote viewers must be authenticated. No public viewer binding by default. Recordings and screenshots remain project-private. A graphical session is not a security sandbox."},{"u":"architecture.html#76-rendering-and-performance","p":"Architecture","h":"7.6 Rendering and Performance","x":"Software rendering must work on CPU-only systems. GPU acceleration should be optional. Do not assume that software-rendered recording can reliably sustain 30 or 60 FPS. Benchmark real capture throughput, latency, memory use, and CPU load. Pixel-perfect reproducibility is not guaranteed across different hardware, fonts, compositors, and GPU drivers."},{"u":"architecture.html#8-resource-management-and-performance","p":"Architecture","h":"8. Resource Management and Performance","x":"Use existing Linux mechanisms wherever possible."},{"u":"architecture.html#81-execution-controls","p":"Architecture","h":"8.1 Execution Controls","x":"systemd services and scopes. cgroups v2. CPU quotas and weights. MemoryHigh and MemoryMax. Process count limits. I/O weighting. PSI-based resource pressure monitoring. Reserve enough resources for host administration and SSH access even during heavy project builds."},{"u":"architecture.html#82-cache-strategy","p":"Architecture","h":"8.2 Cache Strategy","x":"Support: Shared read-only toolchains. Project-private writable package caches. Safe compiler caches. Persistent project-specific language-server state. Selective cache reuse when ownership and integrity are controlled. Do not blindly expose shared writable caches across security boundaries."},{"u":"architecture.html#83-benchmark-requirements","p":"Architecture","h":"8.3 Benchmark Requirements","x":"Measure: Idle OS CPU and memory consumption. Cold and warm project startup. Coding harness startup. Repository indexing. Concurrent builds and tests. Local toolchain installation. Global package promotion. Snapshot and restore latency. GUI session startup. Screenshot latency and video throughput. Host responsiveness under resource pressure. Recovery after reboot, process failure, SSH disconnect, and interrupted package changes. Compare against a minimal Debian baseline using equivalent development tooling. Do not claim performance improvements without measurements."},{"u":"architecture.html#9-networking-and-credential-management","p":"Architecture","h":"9. Networking and Credential Management","x":""},{"u":"architecture.html#91-remote-access","p":"Architecture","h":"9.1 Remote Access","x":"Use OpenSSH with key-based authentication. Prefer private networking such as WireGuard or Tailscale. Hardened deployment defaults: Disable password authentication. Disable remote root login. Restrict public ingress. Explicitly expose development servers when necessary. Keep administrative interfaces private."},{"u":"architecture.html#92-project-networking","p":"Architecture","h":"9.2 Project Networking","x":"Projects may need local servers, databases, browsers, and external development services. Future capabilities should include: Per-project port allocation. Port collision management. Network restrictions for autonomous operations. Controlled access to development services. Optional per-project network namespaces."},{"u":"architecture.html#93-credentials","p":"Architecture","h":"9.3 Credentials","x":"Separate: Host administrator credentials. System-agent credentials. Project credentials. Git credentials. Package registry credentials. Optional external service credentials. Avoid distributing permanent unrestricted host secrets to project agents. Use scoped or short-lived credentials where practical. A secret delivered to an agent process must be treated as visible to that process."},{"u":"architecture.html#94-auditing","p":"Architecture","h":"9.4 Auditing","x":"Record privileged host operations with: Requesting identity. Requested action. Authorization decision. Time. Result. Verification. Recovery reference where applicable. Avoid logging secret values and unnecessary sensitive terminal input."},{"u":"architecture.html#10-distribution-build-and-update-strategy","p":"Architecture","h":"10. Distribution Build and Update Strategy","x":""},{"u":"architecture.html#101-initial-target","p":"Architecture","h":"10.1 Initial Target","x":"Debian 13 Stable. AMD64 first. QEMU/KVM testing. Minimal headless system. Custom Agent OS packages. Installer ISO after the core runtime is validated."},{"u":"architecture.html#102-build-process","p":"Architecture","h":"10.2 Build Process","x":"Construct a minimal Debian environment using standard Debian tooling. Package Agent OS components as .deb packages. Maintain custom systemd units and policy configuration. Produce a VM image. Validate boot, networking, and isolation. Build an installer or live image using an evaluated Debian image workflow. Test clean installation, upgrades, and recovery. Publish versioned, documented build artifacts. Avoid maintaining a fork of the entire Debian package repository."},{"u":"architecture.html#103-update-strategy","p":"Architecture","h":"10.3 Update Strategy","x":"System updates must be controlled through hostd. Requirements: Generate upgrade plans. Detect package conflicts. Identify affected services. Create appropriate recovery points. Request approval for high-risk operations. Apply updates. Validate host health. Reboot only according to policy. Record the result. A successful APT operation does not guarantee a bootable recovery state."},{"u":"architecture.html#104-proposed-repository","p":"Architecture","h":"10.4 Proposed Repository","x":"agent-os/ README.md docs/ ARCHITECTURE.md THREAT_MODEL.md DECISIONS.md TEST_PLAN.md packaging/ debian/ images/ manifests/ crates/ agentos-cli/ workspaced/ hostd/ storage/ gui-backend/ configs/ systemd/ policies/ toolchains/ tests/ integration/ vm/ isolation/ gui/ examples/ project-config.yaml system-change.yaml Do not scaffold components merely because they appear in this conceptual structure. Create them when implementation requires them."},{"u":"architecture.html#11-proposed-configuration-contracts","p":"Architecture","h":"11. Proposed Configuration Contracts","x":"These examples are conceptual and are not yet implemented."},{"u":"architecture.html#111-project-definition","p":"Architecture","h":"11.1 Project Definition","x":"apiVersion: agentos.dev/v1alpha1 kind: Project metadata: name: desktop-app spec: execution: mode: native-restricted harness: pi resources: cpuQuotaPercent: 300 memoryHigh: 6Gi memoryMax: 8Gi storage: persistent: true snapshots: manual-and-boundary toolchains: node: \"24\" graphics: enabled: true backend: auto resolution: 1920x1080 softwareRenderingAllowed: true network: policy: standard-development The harness field is optional and must not couple the OS to one agent implementation."},{"u":"architecture.html#112-system-change","p":"Architecture","h":"11.2 System Change","x":"apiVersion: agentos.dev/v1alpha1 kind: SystemChange metadata: reason: \"Make ripgrep available to all projects\" spec: operations: - type: package.install source: debian-stable name: ripgrep verification: - type: executable.version name: rg authorization: policy: evaluate Avoid accepting unrestricted shell commands in a privileged verification executor. Use typed checks or unprivileged execution whenever possible."},{"u":"architecture.html#12-implementation-roadmap","p":"Architecture","h":"12. Implementation Roadmap","x":""},{"u":"architecture.html#phase-0--research-and-threat-model","p":"Architecture","h":"Phase 0 — Research and Threat Model","x":"[x] Document protected assets, trust boundaries, and attacker capabilities. [x] Determine the exact native isolation design. [x] Verify Debian kernel and systemd capabilities. [x] Establish a reproducible VM development environment. [x] Implement test VM snapshot/reset tooling. [x] Define project filesystem layout and UID ownership. [x] Benchmark minimal Debian as a reference. Exit criterion: documented threat model, baseline VM, and reproducible isolation test procedure."},{"u":"architecture.html#phase-1--native-project-environments","p":"Architecture","h":"Phase 1 — Native Project Environments","x":"[x] Create two independent project identities. [x] Provision private project homes and workspaces. [x] Execute arbitrary native commands as each project identity. [x] Launch at least one supported coding harness. [x] Install a development tool within a project without root. [x] Enforce CPU, memory, and process limits. [x] Implement basic project CLI lifecycle operations. [x] Test filesystem, credential, IPC, and host restrictions. [x] Test persistence after SSH disconnect. [x] Test project cleanup and deletion. Exit criterion: two project agents can operate independently, and neither can read the other's private workspace or alter host packages through normal access paths."},{"u":"architecture.html#phase-2--system-agent-and-global-packages","p":"Architecture","h":"Phase 2 — System Agent and Global Packages","x":"[x] Define typed hostd operation contracts. [x] Implement authenticated caller identity checks. [x] Separate project-agent and system-agent privileges. [x] Execute an approved APT installation. [x] Add change planning and impact reporting. [x] Add authorization and audit logging. [x] Implement global versioned toolchains. [x] Validate project read-only access to global tools. [x] Prove project agents cannot call privileged operations. [x] Test interrupted package transactions. Exit criterion: the system agent can request and perform an approved global ripgrep installation, all projects can use the result, and project agents cannot impersonate the system agent."},{"u":"architecture.html#phase-3--btrfs-workspace-lifecycle","p":"Architecture","h":"Phase 3 — Btrfs Workspace Lifecycle","x":"[x] Implement project subvolume creation. [x] Implement project snapshots. [x] Implement snapshot listing and retention. [x] Implement safe restoration. [x] Validate nested subvolume handling. [x] Add disk-pressure safeguards. [x] Test recovery after destructive edits. [x] Implement an off-host backup prototype. [x] Restore from backup on a fresh VM. Exit criterion: a project can be restored from a local snapshot and independently recovered from external backup."},{"u":"architecture.html#phase-4--on-demand-graphical-testing","p":"Architecture","h":"Phase 4 — On-Demand Graphical Testing","x":"[x] Evaluate waymux on Debian. [x] Evaluate headless Sway or Weston as fallback. [x] Start a GUI session under a project identity. [x] Launch a real GTK, Qt, Tauri, or Electron application. [x] Support keyboard and mouse automation. [x] Capture screenshots. [x] Record video. [x] Save capture metadata. [x] Test graphical session isolation. [x] Test authenticated remote viewing. [x] Test session cleanup. [x] Benchmark software rendering. [x] Document optional GPU requirements. Exit criterion: a project agent can test a real graphical application, capture screenshots and video, and stop the session without affecting another project."},{"u":"architecture.html#phase-5--distribution-packaging","p":"Architecture","h":"Phase 5 — Distribution Packaging","x":"[x] Package Agent OS services and configuration as .deb packages. [x] Build a VM image. [x] Automate boot and installation tests. [x] Create a bootable installer ISO. [x] Implement secure administrator enrollment. [x] Test clean installation. [x] Test normal upgrades. [x] Test system recovery. [x] Test preservation of persistent project data. [x] Document installation and maintenance. [x] Run extended unattended tests with multiple project agents. Exit criterion: Agent OS can be installed, administered remotely, and used for its core workflows without manual post-install workarounds."},{"u":"architecture.html#future-work","p":"Architecture","h":"Future Work","x":"[ ] Container-on-demand execution. [ ] VM-on-demand execution. [ ] GPU-accelerated graphical sessions. [ ] Full GNOME/KDE integration testing. [ ] Per-project network egress policies. [ ] Full bootable system rollback. [ ] Web management UI. [ ] Remote management API. [ ] Multi-host administration. Agent workflow scheduling is intentionally outside the OS core."},{"u":"architecture.html#13-definition-of-done--first-usable-release","p":"Architecture","h":"13. Definition of Done — First Usable Release","x":"A clean installation must demonstrate: Headless operation: System boots without a desktop and supports secure SSH administration. Native execution: Two independently managed projects can launch coding agents. Project isolation: Project A cannot access Project B's private files, IPC resources, or credentials under the tested threat model. Project freedom: A project agent installs its own development tools and builds software without root. Agent-maintained host: A system agent requests an approved global package installation through hostd. Global visibility: Newly installed shared packages become available to authorized projects. Toolchain independence: Projects can use different language versions without modifying each other's environments. Resource protection: Runaway project workloads cannot exhaust the host management reserve under the tested scenarios. Snapshot recovery: A project can recover from destructive modifications. Backup recovery: Project data can be restored on a new VM. Graphical testing: An agent can create an off-screen desktop, launch an application, interact with it, capture screenshots and video, and close the session. Persistence: Project data survives reboot, with process/session recovery following documented lifecycle rules. Repeatability: A newly installed VM passes the same automated acceptance tests."},{"u":"architecture.html#14-open-architecture-questions","p":"Architecture","h":"14. Open Architecture Questions","x":"These decisions must be resolved through prototyping, investigation, or explicit architectural review. A question leaves this list when the decision log in section 15 settles it."},{"u":"architecture.html#host","p":"Architecture","h":"Host","x":"System configuration model. Debian versus Arch if future requirements change."},{"u":"architecture.html#native-execution","p":"Architecture","h":"Native Execution","x":"Required namespace boundaries. IPC and D-Bus isolation. Network policies for autonomous agents."},{"u":"architecture.html#packages-and-credentials","p":"Architecture","h":"Packages and Credentials","x":"Project-scoped alternatives for installers expecting APT. Credential brokering and expiration. Per-project Git and registry authentication. System-agent approval interface."},{"u":"architecture.html#filesystem","p":"Architecture","h":"Filesystem","x":"Compression configuration (recommendation). Backup encryption, schedule, and incremental sends. Host rollback implementation."},{"u":"architecture.html#graphics","p":"Architecture","h":"Graphics","x":"GUI accessibility automation. GPU permissions and isolation (requirements). Automatic retention of recordings."},{"u":"architecture.html#product","p":"Architecture","h":"Product","x":"Final product name. CLI namespace. Service and package naming. Scope of future management UI."},{"u":"architecture.html#15-decision-log","p":"Architecture","h":"15. Decision Log","x":"2026-11-08 Agent OS is a temporary name Avoid premature branding 2026-11-08 Remote coding workstation Prioritize agentic development rather than generic servers 2026-11-08 Native-first execution Preserve conventional Linux compatibility 2026-11-08 Separate system and project agents Enable host maintenance without giving projects root privileges 2026-11-08 Debian Stable preferred Conservative host updates and independent toolchains 2026-11-08 Btrfs preferred Efficient snapshots and workspace lifecycle 2026-11-08 On-demand graphical sessions Support GUI testing without a permanent desktop 2026-11-08 Evaluate existing GUI runtimes Avoid reimplementing compositors and capture systems 2026-11-08 native-restricted uses transient systemd system services in per-project slices, without bubblewrap User managers lack filesystem restrictions, and systemd already builds the namespaces. See doc/ISOLATION.md. 2026-11-08 Project UIDs come from 20000 to 29999 and are never reused Stays clear of adduser and subordinate UID ranges. Snapshots can outlive a project. 2026-11-08 Root owns each project directory, and a per-project tmp directory backs /tmp Blocks symlink swaps against root tooling. Commands in one project share /tmp. 2026-11-08 Development tests run QEMU from a pinned Docker toolbox Pins the VM tooling without host packages. Agent OS itself still needs no container. 2026-11-08 Cross-project abstract sockets and loopback remain accepted gaps Per-project network namespaces are unresolved. See doc/THREAT_MODEL.md. 2026-11-08 agentos is a root-only shell CLI with no daemon Section 4.1 defers a daemon until scripts and systemd units are not enough. systemd already supervises the commands. 2026-11-08 Systemd units are named by project UID Project names can contain hyphens, which mean hierarchy in slice names. 2026-11-08 agentos-project.slice caps all projects at 85% of RAM and 16384 tasks Keeps capacity for SSH and host services under project load. 2026-11-08 Each project gets a network namespace with veth, NAT, and an nftables policy Supersedes the accepted loopback and abstract socket gaps. Egress policy remains future work. See doc/ISOLATION.md. 2026-11-08 hostd is written in Rust and socket-activated per connection A root service that parses requests needs typed input handling. systemd supplies supervision, and no process exists while idle. See doc/HOSTD.md. 2026-11-08 Rust binaries build with Debian's compiler in a container pinned to the APT snapshot Matches the target's glibc and adds no new toolchain source. 2026-11-08 Each project is one Btrfs subvolume, with cache and tmp as nested subvolumes One snapshot covers workspace, home, state, and artifacts atomically. Rebuildable data stays out. See STORAGE.md. 2026-11-08 Restore gives the project an empty cache A compromised agent can poison a cache, and keeping it would carry the poison past the restore. 2026-11-08 One level-1 qgroup per project, with a referenced limit The limit covers all of a project's subvolumes, and snapshots do not count against it. Measured cost is negligible. 2026-11-08 Backups are a stream on standard output, using btrfs send The off-host side pulls them, so a compromised host cannot reach old backups. Adds no dependency. Encryption and incremental sends stay open. 2026-11-08 The system agent is the agentos-system account, outside the aos- namespace No project name can collide with it. 2026-11-08 Nothing is automatic by default, and approval binds to one exact transaction The administrator decides what runs unattended. Later package changes cannot widen an approval. 2026-11-08 Global toolchains come only from a pinned catalog and publish with one rename Removes arbitrary global binaries from the API. Projects never see a partial version. 2026-11-08 Headless Sway is the GUI backend, and waymux and Weston are not Every tool comes from Debian, and every required function passed in a project sandbox. waymux listed no window for a GTK3 application, and Weston has no capture or virtual-input protocol that these tools use. See GUI.md. 2026-11-08 GUI sessions run entirely as the project, with no daemon and no root step Root never touches a project path, and project limits apply to the compositor. 2026-11-08 One supervisor per session owns its state, and the viewer connection provides the input devices Sway's headless seat has no pointer or keyboard until a virtual device attaches, and a new device for each action loses keys. 2026-11-08 Remote viewing is an RFB byte stream through ssh and agentos project gui ... view SSH keys authenticate the viewer, and nothing listens on a port. 2026-11-08 Only --detach commands are guaranteed to survive a lost connection, and nothing resumes after reboot Transient units are not persistent. Data survives. See doc/CLI.md. 2026-11-09 Agent OS ships as one agentos package built with debhelper, and install.sh is gone Every suite now tests what a host installs. Packages cannot own /usr/local, so the programs moved to /usr/sbin and /usr/bin. The components have no separate install need yet. 2026-11-09 Upgrades reload the firewall and restart nothing Each agentos-netns@ instance requires the firewall, so a restart would delete the network of every running project. 2026-11-09 The default policy and storage.conf are copied once and are not dpkg conffiles The administrator owns them after the first install. A changed conffile would stop a non-interactive upgrade with a prompt. 2026-11-09 The package refuses removal while any project exists Removal would strand each project's account, slice, and namespace. The maintainer script is the one place that covers both apt and hostd. 2026-11-09 The VM image is built with mmdebstrap inside the test VM A minimal bootstrap proves the package's Depends, and the build gets root and a real block device without privileged containers on the dev machine. The installer ISO can still choose differently, so section 14 keeps the question open. 2026-11-09 The image has separate root and data partitions, and only the data partition grows A project that fills the Btrfs filesystem cannot fill the root. systemd-repart and x-systemd.growfs do the growth, so no script runs at boot. 2026-11-09 The image holds no login, and the first administrator key arrives as a systemd boot credential No default password or baked key can leak with the image. This adds no new first-boot program. Interactive enrollment for the installer is still open. 2026-11-09 The image boots with BIOS only The test VM's firmware proves it. UEFI arrives with the installer, where it needs real testing, and the bootloader question in section 14 stays open. 2026-11-09 The image is built from the APT snapshot and then points at live Debian The snapshot makes the contents repeatable. A host pinned to it would never receive a security update. 2026-11-09 The installer copies the image's root filesystem from a small live system, instead of driving debian-installer Every install is the system the image suite tested, it needs no network, and the console prompts are ours to test. The cost is that this repository owns partitioning. 2026-11-09 grub-cloud-amd64 is the boot loader, and an installed disk boots under BIOS and UEFI One package writes both, keeps them current on upgrade, and needs no firmware detection. It does nothing without its flag file, so the VM image stays BIOS only. 2026-11-09 Recovery from a broken root is an installer repair that keeps the data partition Projects survive without a recent backup, and the same medium that installs the host repairs it. Accounts and limits are rebuilt from the registry, so the root stays replaceable. Toolchains and administrator config are not preserved, because they live on the root. 2026-11-09 package_upgrade takes no names and always needs approval A host upgrade concerns the whole system, and a partial list could leave it inconsistent. It reuses the exact-archive approval, so a later publication cannot widen what applies. Recovery points stay open, because the ext4 root has no snapshots. 2026-11-09 The installer enrolls one SSH key at the console and generates the host keys No password or network path exists during enrollment, and the administrator can check the host key on the first connection instead of trusting it. The key can be pasted, or read from a USB stick when the console cannot paste."},{"u":"architecture.html#16-upstream-technical-references","p":"Architecture","h":"16. Upstream Technical References","x":"These are starting points for implementation research, not claims that all proposed Agent OS capabilities already exist. Debian Releases Debian 13 Trixie Debian Live Manual Debian debootstrap systemd Execution and Sandboxing systemd Resource Control Bubblewrap Btrfs Documentation waymux waymux Source waymux Architecture Arch Linux System Maintenance"},{"u":"architecture.html#implementation-reminder","p":"Architecture","h":"Implementation Reminder","x":"Build the smallest working native, isolated project execution prototype first. Do not begin by implementing a general agent orchestrator, custom package manager, or custom graphical compositor. The defining capability of Agent OS is: An agent-maintainable Linux workstation with controlled host privileges, native project-isolated execution, and on-demand graphical testing."},{"u":"threat-model.html#agent-os-threat-model","p":"Threat model","h":"Agent OS threat model","x":"Status: Phase 5 complete, reviewed 2026-11-09. Revise this file for every change that touches privilege escalation, credentials, filesystem mounts, networking, or GUI capture. Check names in this document are rows in tests/isolation/checks.tsv. The isolation design explains the controls."},{"u":"threat-model.html#protected-assets","p":"Threat model","h":"Protected assets","x":"A1 Project workspace, home, cache, state, and artifacts Source code and agent memory belong to one project. A2 Project credentials: Git, registry, and API tokens A leak acts with the project's external authority. A3 GUI sockets, recordings, screenshots, and input traces Captures can contain secrets typed on screen. A4 Host configuration, boot files, and system services A change here persists beyond any project. A5 APT and dpkg state, and global toolchains Every project executes this software. A6 Administrator and system-agent credentials, and SSH host keys These grant host control or impersonation. A7 /var/lib/agent-os/metadata and managed snapshots Tampering rewrites identity mappings or recovery data. A8 Audit history Without it, nobody can reconstruct a privileged change. A9 Host availability, including the SSH management reserve A runaway project can lock out the administrator."},{"u":"threat-model.html#attacker-capabilities","p":"Threat model","h":"Attacker capabilities","x":"T1 Compromised or misaligned project agent Runs arbitrary code as its project UID, for as long as it wants. T2 Malicious project dependency, for example an npm post-install script The same capability as T1. T3 Compromised system agent Sends any request that its identity is allowed to send to hostd. It has no root shell. T4 Malicious Debian or toolchain package approved by policy Runs as root during installation. T5 Network attacker Reaches any port that the host exposes. T6 Holder of a kernel exploit Crosses every boundary in this document. A prompt-injected agent is T1 or T3. The design does not try to detect injection. It limits what the affected identity can do."},{"u":"threat-model.html#trust-boundaries","p":"Threat model","h":"Trust boundaries","x":"B1 Project to project Separate UIDs, mode 0700 directories, mount, IPC, and network namespaces, ProtectProc=invisible Tested B2 Project to host No capabilities, no new privileges, read-only system paths, private /run, network namespace, and nftables input policy Tested B3 Project to system agent Separate UID, private /run, socket group, and hostd accepts peers by SO_PEERCRED Tested B4 System agent to root Typed hostd operations, policy, human approval, and audit Tested. Package scripts remain gap 6. B5 Network to host OpenSSH keys only, and no remote root. The VM image enforces this. Private networking is still design only Tested for the VM image B6 Project to kernel Seccomp filter and hidden kernel interfaces reduce exposure only Partial by design"},{"u":"threat-model.html#threats-and-controls","p":"Threat model","h":"Threats and controls","x":"Read another project's files T1, T2 A1, A2 B1 file modes home_read, workspace_read Discover or reuse data in shared temporary storage T1, T2 A1, A2 Per-project /tmp, per-unit /dev/shm tmp_cross, var_tmp_cross, dev_shm_cross Read another project's IPC objects T1, T2 A1 PrivateIPC=yes sysv_shm_cross, mqueue_cross Inspect or signal another project's processes T1, T2 A1, A2 ProtectProc=invisible, UID checks proc_visible, proc_cmdline, proc_environ, signal Connect to another project's GUI socket T1, T2 A3 Sockets live in a mode 0700 directory under the project's own /tmp gui_socket, tests/gui/guest/isolation.sh Connect to another project's X11 display through its abstract socket T1, T2 A3 Abstract sockets belong to the network namespace tests/gui/guest/isolation.sh Log in to a new image with a shipped credential T5 A6 The image has no password and no key, and the first key arrives as a boot credential tests/image/run Log in to a newly installed host with a shipped credential T5 A6 The ISO holds no password or key. The installer refuses to go on without a valid public key tests/installer/run Impersonate a newly installed host on its first connection T5 A6 The installer generates host keys and shows the fingerprint on the console tests/installer/run Keep a stale credential or host key through a repair T5 A6 A repair enrolls the key typed at the console and generates new host keys tests/installer/run Guess a password, or log in remotely as root T5 A6 PasswordAuthentication no, KbdInteractiveAuthentication no, PermitRootLogin no tests/image/guest/first_boot.sh Impersonate another machine built from the same image T5 A6 The image ships no host keys or machine ID, and each first boot generates its own tests/image/run Read another project's screenshots, recordings, or input traces T1, T2 A3 Project directory modes, mode 0600 files tests/gui/guest/isolation.sh Reach a GUI viewer over the network T5 A3 The viewer binds only a project-private Unix socket, and remote use goes through SSH and root tests/gui/run Trick root into opening a project path through a GUI session T1, T2 A4 agentos project gui only starts commands as the project, and root opens no project file tests/gui/guest/cleanup.sh Keep a graphical process after a session ends T1, T2 A9 Applications run in the supervisor's process groups, and the compositor dies with its supervisor tests/gui/guest/cleanup.sh Keep typed secrets in the input trace T1, T2 A3 The trace stores the length of typed text, and <char> for printable keys tests/gui/guest/agent.sh Reach another project's sockets or ports T1, T2 A1, A3 Per-project network namespace, nftables forward policy abstract_socket, loopback_tcp, tests/integration/guest/network.sh Reach services on the host, or change the project network T1, T2 A4, A6 Network namespace, nftables input policy, no capabilities host_loopback_ssh, host_uplink_ssh, net_admin, tests/integration/guest/network.sh Modify host files or packages T1, T2 A4, A5 ProtectSystem=strict, file modes etc_write, usr_write, boot_write, dpkg_db_write, apt_install, toolchain_write Escalate through setuid, sudo, or D-Bus T1, T2 A4, A6 NoNewPrivileges=yes, private /run setuid_binary, sudo, system_bus, capabilities, service_start Trick root tooling with a swapped project path T1, T2 A4, A7 Root owns each project directory replace_own_project_dir Run the administrator CLI from a project T1, T2 A4, A7 Root check, no setuid, no sudo agentos_cli Inject through names or sizes given to the root CLI T1, T2 through a relayed request A4 Strict patterns before any use tests/integration/guest/lifecycle.sh Keep files or processes after delete, or redirect it with symlinks and subvolumes T1, T2 A1, A4 Mask the slice, stop it, kill the UID, confirm no process remains, remove with no symlink following tests/integration/guest/delete.sh Inherit another account by name collision Administrator error A4 create refuses any account that it did not record allocating tests/integration/guest/delete.sh Read host credentials or metadata T1, T2 A6, A7 File modes, ProtectHome=yes, masked /var/lib/agent-os shadow, ssh_host_key, admin_ssh_keys, metadata_read, snapshots_list Change kernel state T1, T2 A4, A9 Protect settings, no capabilities dmesg, sysctl_write, cgroup_write, mount_tmpfs, block_device Exhaust host memory or tasks T1, T2 A9 Per-project slice limits memory_limit, tasks_limit Call hostd or read its state from a project T1, T2 A4, A5, A8 Private /run, socket mode, SO_PEERCRED, file modes hostd_socket, hostd_client, hostd_state_read, tests/hostd/guest/privileges.sh Impersonate the system agent or administrator T1, T2, T3 A4, A5 Identity comes only from the kernel, and unknown fields are rejected tests/hostd/guest/privileges.sh Approve one's own change T3 A4, A5 Only peer UID 0 can decide tests/hostd/guest/packages.sh Remove the broker's own package T3 A4, A5, A9 Removal always needs human approval, agentos is a protected package, and prerm refuses while any project exists tests/hostd/guest/packages.sh, tests/packaging/guest/lifecycle.sh Leave the host forwarding packets after Agent OS is removed T5 A4 Stopping the firewall turns forwarding off before it deletes the policy tests/packaging/guest/lifecycle.sh Change the transaction or package contents after approval T3, T4 A4, A5 Apply installs only the hashed archives kept by the plan, and compares a new simulation tests/hostd/guest/packages.sh, tests/hostd/guest/toolchains.sh Fill the broker's connection slots T3 A9 One time limit per request, refused lock waits, separate administrator socket tests/hostd/guest/privileges.sh Smuggle apt options, paths, regexes, or removal suffixes through a name T3 A4, A5 Character check, and apt must act on exactly the requested names tests/hostd/guest/packages.sh Get a risky package through the automatic list T3 A4, A5 Automatic only for clean installs with no unit, setuid file, or protected name tests/hostd/guest/packages.sh Publish a tampered or partial toolchain T3, T4 A5 Catalog digest, staged check as nobody, one rename tests/hostd/guest/toolchains.sh, tests/hostd/guest/interrupt.sh Replace a shared toolchain from a project T1, T2 A5 ProtectSystem=strict, root-owned files toolchain_write, tests/hostd/guest/toolchains.sh Read project data as the system agent T3 A1, A2 Project modes, and broker instances and their package scripts have no project data mounted tests/hostd/guest/privileges.sh, tests/hostd/guest/interrupt.sh Upgrade the host without a human, or widen an approved upgrade afterward T3, T4 A4, A5 package_upgrade always needs approval, applies only the hashed archives, and fails if SSH or the firewall is down afterward tests/hostd/guest/upgrade.sh Hide a privileged change T3 A8 Root-only log, synced before each privileged step tests/hostd/guest/packages.sh Leave the host broken by interrupting a transaction T3, power loss A5, A9 Durable applying record, repair before the next change tests/hostd/guest/interrupt.sh, tests/hostd/guest/after_power_loss.sh Read or change managed snapshots T1, T2 A1, A7 Mode 0700, masked /var/lib/agent-os, read-only subvolumes snapshots_list, tests/storage/guest/snapshots.sh Fill the disk past a project's limit T1, T2 A9 One qgroup per project, and no capability to change it tests/storage/guest/layout.sh Escape the limit with a new subvolume T1, T2 A9 The storage check enrolls new subvolumes every minute tests/storage/guest/layout.sh Forge btrfs output with a crafted subvolume name, to affect another project T1, T2 A9 The kernel confirms each ID's path, and parsed output holds no project names tests/storage/guest/layout.sh Survive a restore through a poisoned cache or a running process T1, T2 A1 Restore kills the UID's processes and gives empty cache and tmp tests/storage/guest/restore.sh Use a damaged or crafted backup to gain root or take over a UID Holder of the backup store A1, A4 Checksum before parsing, strict header, btrfs receive --chroot, UID range and collision checks, and refusal of foreign owners, setuid files, and device nodes tests/storage/run"},{"u":"threat-model.html#accepted-gaps","p":"Threat model","h":"Accepted gaps","x":"Each gap stays open until a later change closes it. The suite marks the first one as gap, so it fails when the behavior changes. /etc/passwd reveals project names. passwd_project_names proves the access. Project names must not contain secrets. Network egress is unrestricted. T1 can send any readable project data to the internet. NAT also lets it reach the LAN and cloud metadata addresses. Per-project egress policy is future work. User namespaces stay enabled. user_namespace requires them, because Chromium and Electron sandboxes need them in Phase 4. They add kernel attack surface for T6. The kernel is shared. T6 defeats the design. Use the vm execution profile for workloads that warrant a separate kernel. Same-project processes are not isolated from each other. An agent can read every credential that its project holds. A secret delivered to a project is visible to all of that project's code. Package installation scripts run as root. T4 has full control during installation. Policy and approval reduce the chance of T4, and they do not contain it. A script can also add file capabilities, which the content scan cannot see. Projects can still fill the shared Btrfs filesystem together. The default limit is 80% for each project, so several projects can exceed the disk. A project can also write outside its limit in the time before the storage check enrolls a new subvolume, at most one minute. Under critical pressure the check stops the largest project, which can be the wrong one. Snapshots are not counted against a project's limit. A project can create its own Btrfs subvolumes. systemd cannot filter ioctl arguments, so the sandbox cannot stop this. Snapshots omit their contents, and the snapshot reports how many it omitted. A project that creates shared subvolumes also forces quota rescans, and limits are not exact during a rescan. Project output can contain terminal escape sequences. run, shell, and logs pass the output to the administrator's terminal unfiltered. Project output goes to the host journal. journald's size limits apply, and the journal keeps secrets that a project prints. A project can also push out older host log entries. The system agent can grow the audit log. T3 can send refused requests until the data disk fills. Log rotation does not exist yet. Projects cannot reach this path. A busy lock can delay the administrator. T3 can keep requesting long plans, so an administrator's change can be refused as busy. Stopping agentos-hostd.socket ends this. A human approves what change show displays. The plan lists actions, units, and setuid files. It cannot judge whether a package is trustworthy. Backups are not encrypted or signed. A stream holds every project secret. The checksum catches damage, and someone who can rewrite the backup can also rewrite the checksum. The checks at recover limit what a forged backup can do, but the forged project data is still restored. Encrypt and authenticate the stream outside agentos. agentos does not schedule or rotate backups. The operator pulls them. A backup is always a full stream. A GUI capture holds whatever was on screen. Screenshots and recordings stay in the project and enter its snapshots and backups. agentos-gui rm removes them from the live tree only, and nothing expires them automatically. Graphical sessions do not contain the applications in them. Applications in one session can capture each other and inject input through the compositor. A process that leaves its process group stays alive after stop, until its project command ends. A viewer can send input. An administrator who opens view can click and type in the session, like the project itself can. A leaked connection keeps its identity. SO_PEERCRED describes who connected. If the system agent passes an open socket to another process, that process acts as the system agent. The first boot trusts whoever supplies the boot credential. Until an administrator key exists, anyone who controls the hypervisor can enroll one. That party can already read the disk, and the file is never overwritten once it exists. An installed host already has a key, so this applies to the VM image. The admin account has passwordless sudo. A stolen administrator key is root on the host. The account has no password to type, and the image relies on the key alone. The installer ISO is not signed. Its checksum catches damage, and someone who can replace the ISO can replace the checksum too. Secure Boot must be off to start it. The installer also runs as root on the console, so anyone at the console before the disk is chosen can install their own key."},{"u":"threat-model.html#rules-for-later-phases","p":"Threat model","h":"Rules for later phases","x":"hostd listens only on a Unix socket. File ownership and SO_PEERCRED cannot check a network peer. Root-run tooling must not open a project path through a systemd StandardOutput=file: setting. PID 1 would follow a symlink that the project planted. Host services must not trust a connection because it came from 127.0.0.1. Root-run tooling must not follow symlinks inside a project directory. Bind any GUI viewer to a project-private socket, never to a host port by default."},{"u":"isolation.html#native-isolation-design","p":"Isolation","h":"Native isolation design","x":"Status: implemented by agentos for the native-restricted profile. The test suites pass on Debian 13.7 with systemd 257. The threat model records why each control exists."},{"u":"isolation.html#execution-model","p":"Isolation","h":"Execution model","x":"Each project command runs as a transient systemd system service named aos-run-UID-ID.service, started with systemd-run --uid=aos-NAME. The service joins the slice agentos-project-UID.slice. Use system services instead of user services, because systemd limits filesystem restrictions in user-managed units. A system service also survives SSH disconnects without loginctl enable-linger. Resource limits belong on the slice, so that they cover all of a project's commands together. Unit names use the UID because a hyphen in a slice name means a parent slice, and project names can contain hyphens. The hyphens in agentos-project-UID.slice make every project a child of agentos-project.slice. That parent caps all projects together at 85% of RAM and 16384 tasks, which keeps capacity for SSH and host services. Sandbox settings have one source, configs/systemd/native-restricted.properties. Bubblewrap is not part of this profile. systemd already builds the namespaces, so a second tool would duplicate them."},{"u":"isolation.html#project-identity","p":"Isolation","h":"Project identity","x":"User and group name aos-NAME UID and GID Equal, allocated from 20000 to 29999 Login shell /usr/sbin/nologin Password None Supplementary groups None The range stays inside Debian's normal user range (UID_MIN 1000, UID_MAX 60000), and away from accounts that adduser allocates from 1000. It also stays below SUB_UID_MIN (100000), so a project UID cannot fall inside the subordinate range of a future rootless container. agentos allocates UIDs from metadata/next-uid and never reuses a value. It records each allocation in metadata/projects/NAME.creating before it creates the account. Only a create with that record can resume, so agentos never adopts an unrelated aos-NAME account. Snapshots and backups can outlive a project, and a reused UID would own the old project's restored files. Limit NAME to 28 characters so that aos-NAME fits the 32-character Linux user name limit."},{"u":"isolation.html#project-network","p":"Isolation","h":"Project network","x":"Each project has one network namespace, /run/netns/aos-UID, shared by all of its commands. agentos-netns@UID.service creates it on first use and again after a reboot. Commands in one project reach each other on 127.0.0.1. Projects cannot reach each other's loopback ports or abstract sockets. Each project owns one /30 in 10.213.0.0/16, calculated from its UID. The host side of the veth pair is .1, and the project is .2. agentos project inspect prints the project address. configs/nftables/agentos.nft applies this policy to IPv4 and IPv6: Project to the internet or LAN Allowed, with masquerade Project to another project Dropped Project to any host address Dropped Host to a project port Allowed Internet or LAN to a project Dropped Forwarding unrelated to projects Dropped The firewall unit turns on net.ipv4.ip_forward after it loads the policy. The last rule keeps the host from acting as a router. A host that must also route other traffic, for example for rootful Docker, needs a changed policy. Projects have no IPv6 route. DNS uses the upstream servers in /run/systemd/resolve/resolv.conf, because the systemd-resolved stub listens on the host's loopback."},{"u":"isolation.html#filesystem-layout","p":"Isolation","h":"Filesystem layout","x":"/opt/agent-os/toolchains root:root 0755 Shared toolchains, read-only to projects /var/lib/agent-os root:root 0711 Btrfs mount point, no listing for projects /var/lib/agent-os/metadata root:root 0700 Project definitions, hostd changes and audit log /var/lib/agent-os/system-agent agentos-system 0700 System agent home /var/lib/agent-os/snapshots root:root 0700 Managed snapshots /var/lib/agent-os/projects root:root 0711 Project parent, no listing for projects projects/NAME root:aos-NAME 0750 Project root, a Btrfs subvolume projects/NAME/workspace aos-NAME 0700 Repository checkout, default working directory projects/NAME/home aos-NAME 0700 HOME and project-local tools projects/NAME/cache aos-NAME 0700 XDG_CACHE_HOME, private package caches, a nested subvolume projects/NAME/state aos-NAME 0700 XDG_STATE_HOME, agent state projects/NAME/artifacts aos-NAME 0700 GUI captures and test output. artifacts/gui/ is described in GUI.md. projects/NAME/tmp aos-NAME 0700 Bound over /tmp and /var/tmp, a nested subvolume Root owns projects/NAME so that a project cannot replace workspace with a symlink. tmp is an addition to the architecture layout. Commands in one project share /tmp through it, which a per-unit PrivateTmp= would prevent. STORAGE.md describes subvolumes, snapshots, quotas, and backups."},{"u":"isolation.html#sandbox-settings","p":"Isolation","h":"Sandbox settings","x":"\"Sole control\" means that removing the setting made the suite fail, so no other layer covers that behavior. Privilege NoNewPrivileges, empty CapabilityBoundingSet, RestrictSUIDSGID Setuid binaries do not raise privilege. NoNewPrivileges: sole control for setuid_binary Host paths ProtectSystem=strict, ProtectHome=yes System paths are read-only and homes are hidden. Not run Agent OS state TemporaryFileSystem=/var/lib/agent-os:ro, BindPaths=@ROOT@ Other projects are absent from the mount namespace. Suite still passes because file modes also block access. This layer is defense in depth. Temporary storage BindPaths=@ROOT@/tmp:/tmp and /var/tmp Each project has its own temporary directories. Holder fails to start, and the suite reports the failure Shared memory TemporaryFileSystem=/dev/shm:mode=1777 Each unit has a private /dev/shm. Sole control for dev_shm_cross. PrivateDevices does not isolate /dev/shm. IPC PrivateIPC=yes SysV IPC and POSIX queues are private. Sole control for sysv_shm_cross and mqueue_cross Runtime sockets TemporaryFileSystem=/run, BindReadOnlyPaths=-/run/systemd/resolve D-Bus, hostd, and host sockets are absent, and DNS still works. Sole control for system_bus Processes ProtectProc=invisible Other users' processes are hidden. Sole control for proc_visible and proc_cmdline Network NetworkNamespacePath, Requires, After Each project has its own interfaces and loopback. Sole control for abstract_socket, loopback_tcp, host_loopback_ssh, and host_uplink_ssh Kernel Protect, SystemCallFilter, PrivateDevices Kernel interfaces and devices are hidden. Not run MemoryDenyWriteExecute stays off, because JIT runtimes such as Node.js need writable and executable memory (jit_rwx_memory). RestrictNamespaces stays off, because browser sandboxes need user namespaces (user_namespace). /dev/shm and SysV IPC are private per command. Two commands in one project cannot share them. Pi completes a full tool-call loop under this restriction. Other harnesses are untested."},{"u":"hostd.html#host-maintenance-broker","p":"hostd","h":"Host-maintenance broker","x":"Status: implemented by agentos-hostd. The test procedure covers this contract on Debian 13.7. hostd lets a non-root system agent change the host through typed, policy-checked, audited operations. It has no way to run an arbitrary command."},{"u":"hostd.html#identities","p":"hostd","h":"Identities","x":"Administrator Peer UID 0 All System agent Peer UID of the agentos-system account All except change_approve and change_reject Any other UID No match None. The attempt is audited. hostd reads the peer UID with SO_PEERCRED, which the kernel records at connect time. No request field can state an identity, and any unknown field makes the request malformed. The system agent uses /run/agentos/hostd.sock, root:agentos-system, mode 0660, limited to 16 connections. The administrator uses /run/agentos/hostd-admin.sock, root:root, mode 0600, limited to 4 connections, so the system agent cannot fill it. agentos system chooses the socket by its effective UID. Project sandboxes mount an empty /run, so a project sees neither. The agentos-system account has a home at /var/lib/agent-os/system-agent, no sudo rights, and no group shared with any project. Start a harness under it with sudo -iu agentos-system."},{"u":"hostd.html#service-model","p":"hostd","h":"Service model","x":"systemd listens on the socket and starts one agentos-hostd@.service instance per connection, so no broker process exists while idle. Each instance serves one request and exits. A client that disconnects does not cancel a change. Mutating requests take an exclusive flock. The kernel releases it when the holder dies, so a change found in applying while the lock is held has no live owner. A request that cannot get the lock in 5 seconds is refused as busy. Run it again later."},{"u":"hostd.html#requests","p":"hostd","h":"Requests","x":"Send one JSON object and one newline, up to 64 KiB. The whole request must arrive within 10 seconds. The reply is one JSON object with status set to ok or refused. system_status None Reports dpkg consistency, changes by state, toolchains, and whether a reboot is pending. change_history None Returns the last 200 audit events. change_show id Returns one change. change_plan reason, operation Computes impact, evaluates policy, and records the change. change_approve, change_reject id Records the administrator's decision. change_apply id Applies an approved change and verifies it. recover None Finishes any interrupted change. Every mutating request also does this first. package_install names, up to 32 package_remove names, up to 32 package_upgrade None toolchain_install_global name, version toolchain_remove_global name, version ServiceEnableDisable, UpgradePlan, and recovery-point operations do not exist yet."},{"u":"hostd.html#change-states","p":"hostd","h":"Change states","x":"+--> awaiting_approval --approve--> approved --apply--> applying --> applied | failed change_plan ----+ | | +--> approved (policy) reject plan changed v v rejected stale A plan runs apt-get update, simulates the transaction, and downloads the packages. It copies each archive into metadata/hostd/artifacts/ID, then hashes and lists that copy without running its scripts. It records each package action, each archive's SHA-256 and architecture, the download size, autostart files, and setuid files. Approval covers the exact archives. change_apply hashes the copies and simulates again with them. A changed file or a different transaction moves the change to stale. Request a new plan in that case. The install uses only those copies and downloads nothing, so a repository that republishes a version cannot change what was approved. The copies are deleted when the change ends. For a toolchain, approval covers the whole catalog entry, including its URL, digest, and check. A change is applied only if the command succeeded and every planned action reached its planned version. An upgrade also needs ssh.service and agentos-firewall.service active afterward. An upgrade moves every installed package to its candidate and installs any new package that needs, such as a new kernel. It never removes a package. A package that apt will not move, usually because of a conflict, appears in impact.kept_back and in the decision's reasons. Approval still covers the exact archives, so a version published after approval does not change what applies. reboot_required in system_status is true when a newer kernel file than the running kernel is in /boot. hostd never reboots the host."},{"u":"hostd.html#policy","p":"hostd","h":"Policy","x":"The administrator owns /etc/agent-os/hostd-policy.json. The package copies the default only when the file is absent, so an upgrade preserves it. A missing or malformed policy refuses every plan. automatic_packages Package names that may install without approval. Empty by default. automatic_toolchains name/version entries that may install without approval. Empty by default. protected_package_prefixes Package name prefixes that always need approval. A package install is automatic only when all of these are true: Every requested name is on automatic_packages. The transaction changes or removes no installed package. No package in the transaction matches a protected prefix. No package ships a setuid file, or any file in a systemd unit or generator directory or /etc/init.d. Upgrades and removals always need approval. Removing an essential package is refused outright. Installs pass --no-remove, so an install can never remove a package."},{"u":"hostd.html#package-names","p":"hostd","h":"Package names","x":"The default protected_package_prefixes includes agentos, so a plan that changes the broker's own package always names it as protected. An approved removal still fails while any project exists, because the package's prerm script refuses it. A name must match Debian's characters, without , /, :, or =. . and + are legal in Debian names and are also regex syntax for apt, and apt reads a name- suffix as a removal. A plan therefore stands only if apt acted on exactly the requested names. Anything else is refused as unknown_package. Packages come only from the APT sources already on the host. No operation adds a source."},{"u":"hostd.html#toolchains","p":"hostd","h":"Toolchains","x":"Only entries in /usr/share/agent-os/toolchains.json can be installed. Each entry pins a URL, a SHA-256 digest, and a check command with its expected output. An install runs these steps under /opt/agent-os/toolchains/.staging: Download over HTTPS and compare the digest. Extract without preserving owners or modes. Give all files to root, remove group and other write, and clear setuid and setgid bits. Run the check as nobody with no new privileges. Sync the filesystem, so the files are on disk before they can be published. Rename the tree to /opt/agent-os/toolchains/NAME/VERSION. The rename is the only publication step, so projects see all of a version or none of it. Each project selects versions independently with agentos project toolchain. A project with no selection gets no toolchain on PATH."},{"u":"hostd.html#audit","p":"hostd","h":"Audit","x":"/var/lib/agent-os/metadata/hostd/audit.jsonl is mode 0600. Each line records the time, the event, and the caller's UID, PID, and name. Events also carry the change, action, decision, result, verification, and undo command where they apply. Each event is written and synced before the step it describes or the step that follows it. Planning and repair run privileged tools, so planning and recovery_started come first. If a write fails, the request stops, so no privileged step runs without its record. Malformed requests are recorded without their body, because a body can hold anything a caller sent. denied A caller lacks the role for a request. malformed_request A request cannot be parsed. plan_refused Validation or apt rejects a plan. planning A plan is about to run apt. planned A change is recorded, with its decision and impact. approved, rejected The administrator decides. apply_refused, stale A change cannot apply. applying A change is about to run. finished A change ends, with its verification and undo command. recovery_started A repair is about to run. recovery A repair has run, with its result."},{"u":"hostd.html#recovery","p":"hostd","h":"Recovery","x":"A change left in applying was interrupted by a crash, a kill, or power loss. The next mutating request repairs it before doing anything else. agentos system recover asks for this directly. Package dpkg --configure -a, then apt-get -f --no-remove --no-download install if it touches only approved packages applied if verification passes, otherwise failed Toolchain Removes the staging files applied if the rename happened, otherwise failed Recovery rolls forward. It does not undo a change that had already finished unpacking. Each outcome records the command that would undo the change. An outcome of failed with dpkg_consistent false in status needs an administrator."},{"u":"storage.html#storage-design","p":"Storage","h":"Storage design","x":"Status: implemented by agentos for Phase 3. tests/storage/run passes on Debian 13.7 with kernel 6.12 and btrfs-progs 6.14. /var/lib/agent-os must be on Btrfs, and the package enables quotas on it."},{"u":"storage.html#subvolumes","p":"Storage","h":"Subvolumes","x":"projects/NAME Subvolume Boundary of the snapshot workspace, home, state, artifacts Directories Yes cache, tmp Nested subvolumes No One snapshot of projects/NAME is a single point in time for everything it holds, so it does not need commands to stop. It has the state that a power loss would leave for a running command. A Btrfs snapshot leaves out nested subvolumes, and that is how cache and tmp stay out. A subvolume that a project creates is left out in the same way. The snapshot prints a warning, and agentos project snapshots shows how many it omitted. After a restore, an omitted subvolume is an empty directory."},{"u":"storage.html#snapshots","p":"Storage","h":"Snapshots","x":"Each snapshot is snapshots/NAME/ID/, with a read-only tree subvolume and a meta file. Renaming a staging directory into place publishes it, so a crash never leaves a partial snapshot in the list. manual agentos project snapshot Kept until snapshot-delete auto --auto, for task boundaries, and every restore The newest keep_automatic per project"},{"u":"storage.html#restore","p":"Storage","h":"Restore","x":"restore works in this order: Hide the project from run and record the target snapshot. Mask the slice and kill every process of the UID. Take an automatic pre-restore snapshot, so that the restore can be undone. Build a writable tree from the snapshot, with empty cache and tmp. Swap the trees with two renames, and remove the old tree. The set of paths that exist identifies the step that a failed restore reached, so running it again finishes the work. A rerun must name the same snapshot. Projects get an empty cache on purpose, because a compromised agent can poison one. The empty cache costs a rebuild."},{"u":"storage.html#disk-pressure","p":"Storage","h":"Disk pressure","x":"Project limit --disk, default 80% of the filesystem Writes fail with EDQUOT. Enrollment Every minute New subvolumes join their project's limit. Snapshot refusal Free space below reserve_percent (10) snapshot fails. A restore's safety snapshot still runs. Pruning Free space below reserve_percent Each project keeps one automatic snapshot. Stopping Free space below critical_percent (5) after pruning The commands of the largest project stop. /etc/agent-os/storage.conf holds the three settings. The limit counts what a project references, so a snapshot does not use up its project's limit. Only the free-space checks cover snapshot growth."},{"u":"storage.html#backups","p":"Storage","h":"Backups","x":"agentos project backup writes one stream to standard output: a text header, a btrfs send stream, and a SHA-256 trailer. In this prototype, another machine pulls the backup: ssh admin@host 'sudo agentos project backup my-app --snapshot ID' | age -r RECIPIENT > my-app.backup.age Pulling means that a compromised host cannot reach older backups. The stream is always full, and it needs encrypting outside agentos. agentos project recover keeps the project's UID, limits, and toolchain selection. A selected toolchain must be installed again before commands can run. It spools the stream to disk and checks the trailer before anything parses the stream, so it needs free space for one extra copy. It refuses a name, account, or UID that is already in use."},{"u":"storage.html#measurements","p":"Storage","h":"Measurements","x":"From tests/storage/benchmark on 2026-11-08, in the baseline VM. The corpus has 15128 entries and 185 MiB. Each value is a median, and few snapshots exist during the runs. No quota, no compression 426 939 688 211 Quota, no compression 423 937 644 210 Quota, zstd:1 679 934 714 117 No quota, zstd:1 670 938 734 117 Snapshot 44 Restore, including the pre-restore snapshot 784 Backup to a file, 182 MiB 844 Quotas have no measurable cost at this scale. The Btrfs documentation warns that their cost grows with the number of snapshots, and this benchmark does not cover that."},{"u":"storage.html#open-decisions","p":"Storage","h":"Open decisions","x":"Compression Mount with compress=zstd:1. It saved 44% and made unpacking about 60% slower. The mount belongs to the installer. Deferred past Phase 5: the installer does not set it yet. Backup encryption, signing, schedule, and incremental sends Compare restic with btrfs send -p once a real backup target exists. Deferred past Phase 5: no real backup target exists yet. Automatic snapshots at task boundaries Have a harness or the system agent call snapshot --auto. Nothing calls it yet. First harness integration"},{"u":"gui.html#graphical-sessions","p":"GUI","h":"Graphical sessions","x":"Status: implemented for Phase 4. tests/gui/run passes on Debian 13.7 with kernel 6.12, Sway 1.10.1, and software rendering. A project agent can open an off-screen Wayland desktop, run a real application in it, drive that application with a mouse and keyboard, and keep screenshots and video. Nothing graphical runs while no session exists."},{"u":"gui.html#install-the-packages","p":"GUI","h":"Install the packages","x":"The GUI tools are ordinary host packages, so they go through the hostd approval path: mapfile -t packages < /usr/share/agent-os/gui-packages agentos system package install \"${packages[@]}\" --reason \"graphical sessions\" agentos system change approve ID agentos system change apply ID"},{"u":"gui.html#commands","p":"GUI","h":"Commands","x":"A project agent runs agentos-gui directly, from inside its sandbox. An administrator runs the same subcommands as agentos project gui NAME SUBCOMMAND .... start ID [--size WxH] Starts a session and returns when it is ready. The default size is 1280x720. run ID -- COMMAND [ARG...] Starts an application in the session and prints its number. It uses the caller's directory and environment. wait ID --ready Waits for the session to be ready. wait ID --window APP_ID Waits for a window. windows prints each window's app_id. wait ID --app NUMBER Waits for an application to exit and returns its exit status. windows ID Prints one JSON object per window. type ID TEXT Types printable ASCII, newlines, and tabs. Other text is refused. key ID KEY Presses a key or combination, such as Return or Control+a. move ID X Y Moves the pointer. click ID X Y [--button left|middle|right] Clicks at a position. screenshot ID [--output FILE] Captures the screen and prints the path. record start ID, record stop ID Records the screen. A session keeps one recording. result ID pass|fail [--detail TEXT] Stores a test result in the metadata. status ID Prints the metadata. view ID Connects standard input and output to the session's RFB viewer. list Prints each session's ID, state, size, and start time. stop ID Stops the session and prints its final state. rm ID Deletes a stopped session's artifacts. ID matches ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$. An ID stays taken until rm removes it, so a new run cannot overwrite an earlier run's captures. The agentos-gui exit status is 0 on success, 1 on refusal, and 2 on invalid usage. wait --app returns the application's status instead."},{"u":"gui.html#session-lifetime","p":"GUI","h":"Session lifetime","x":"A session started with agentos project gui NAME start has its own systemd unit, and lasts until stop, agentos project stop, or a reboot. A session started with agentos-gui start belongs to the command that started it, and ends with that command. Running agentos project run NAME -- agentos-gui start ID on its own therefore starts a session that ends at once. Use the agentos project gui form from outside a project. stop Finishes any recording, stops applications, and records stopped. agentos project stop or the starting command exits The same. Supervisor killed, or host reboot The compositor dies with its supervisor. The next list or start records orphaned and clears the runtime directory. agentos project restore or delete Stops the session with the project's other commands."},{"u":"gui.html#artifacts","p":"GUI","h":"Artifacts","x":"artifacts/gui/ID/ metadata.json screenshots/0001.png recording.mkv inputs.jsonl logs/ metadata.json records the project, session, backend, resolution, state, timestamps, each application's command and exit status, every capture, the recording's codec, and the test result. inputs.jsonl keeps the positions of pointer actions and the names of special keys. Typed text can be a secret, so the trace stores only its length, and a printable key in a combination appears as <char>. The artifacts are part of the project's snapshots and backups. rm removes them from the live tree only."},{"u":"gui.html#view-a-session-remotely","p":"GUI","h":"View a session remotely","x":"view carries RFB over standard input and output, so OpenSSH authenticates the viewer and no port opens on the host. This command gives a local VNC client a port on 127.0.0.1:5900: socat TCP-LISTEN:5900,bind=127.0.0.1,reuseaddr \\ EXEC:\"ssh ADMIN@HOST sudo agentos project gui NAME view ID\" A viewer can send input to the session."},{"u":"gui.html#design","p":"GUI","h":"Design","x":"Each session has one supervisor process, which runs as the project. It starts dbus-daemon, sway, and wayvnc, and it holds the session's only control socket. Every command is a request to that socket, so metadata.json has one writer. A session is alive exactly when its control socket answers, which the kernel settles without a PID file. Runtime sockets live in /tmp/gui/ID, which is the project's own /tmp, mode 0700, and left out of snapshots. Applications run in their own process groups as children of the supervisor. Root never opens a project path. agentos project gui only starts agentos-gui as the project, by the same path as agentos project run. The supervisor keeps one RFB connection to wayvnc open for the life of the session. Sway's headless seat reports no pointer or keyboard until a virtual device attaches, so a client without one gets no input at all. Tools that create a device per action, such as wtype, also lose the first key while the application loads the new keymap. One long-lived connection removes both problems. wf-recorder writes the complete file on SIGINT, and then waits for one more compositor frame before it exits. On a static screen that frame never arrives. record stop moves the pointer by one pixel to produce it, and kills the recorder after five seconds if it still has not exited."},{"u":"gui.html#backend-evaluation","p":"GUI","h":"Backend evaluation","x":"Each candidate ran in a project sandbox on the test VM, with no GPU. Debian package Yes No Yes Starts in the sandbox Yes Yes Yes Lists a GTK3 window Yes No, the window list stayed empty Not tested Screenshot Yes, grim No, it reported no windows No, grim needs wlr-screencopy Video Yes, wf-recorder No, stop wrote no file Not tested Input Yes, with a virtual device Not reached No, wayvnc needs the virtual pointer protocol Viewer without a GPU Yes, wayvnc The default viewer needs a GPU, by its documentation No Sway is the backend because every tool comes from the Debian archive and every requirement passed. The cause of waymux's empty window list is unknown. waymux needs Rust 1.88, newer than Debian's compiler, and has no committed Cargo.lock or GitHub release. Its built-in capture and input would remove the viewer connection and the recorder workaround, so reevaluate it once it publishes releases. XWayland runs inside each Sway session, and the suite runs a GTK application through it. That covers the Xvfb fallback's purpose, so Xvfb is not used."},{"u":"gui.html#software-rendering-benchmark","p":"GUI","h":"Software rendering benchmark","x":"tests/gui/benchmark printed these values on 2026-11-08, on a 4-vCPU KVM guest with 4 GiB of RAM and WLR_RENDERER=pixman. Session start 239 ms 236 ms GTK window after run 175 ms 177 ms Idle CPU, one window 0% of one core 0% of one core Session memory, RSS 86 MiB 100 MiB Screenshot 59 ms 87 ms Recording, 40-pixel moving bar: frames per second 31 31 Recording, 40-pixel moving bar: CPU of one core 25% 53% Recording, 40-pixel moving bar: KiB per minute 257 279 Recording, every pixel changing: frames per second 31 30 Recording, every pixel changing: CPU of one core 46% 100% Recording, every pixel changing: KiB per minute 18042 40882 The test application animates at 30 frames per second, so those rows show that recording kept up at that rate, on an otherwise idle guest. A loaded host or a faster application can drop frames. CPU counts every process in the session, including the application."},{"u":"gui.html#optional-gpu-acceleration","p":"GUI","h":"Optional GPU acceleration","x":"This is a requirements list. No part of it has been tested. The native-restricted profile sets PrivateDevices=yes, which hides /dev/dri. A GPU session needs a profile that exposes one render node, with DeviceAllow= and access through the render group. A shared GPU does not isolate one project's memory from another's the way separate UIDs do. Exposing it adds kernel and driver attack surface, so it needs a threat-model revision before it ships. The supervisor sets WLR_RENDERER=pixman and LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1. A GPU backend must remove both. wf-recorder encodes with libx264 on the CPU. Hardware encoding needs a VA-API driver for the GPU, and a codec that the driver supports."},{"u":"gui.html#limits","p":"GUI","h":"Limits","x":"type covers the US layout only, because wayvnc maps key symbols through that layout. run starts applications with no accessibility bus, so accessibility-tree automation is not available. Only application mode exists. Desktop and isolated session modes remain optional work. The threat model lists the accepted gaps for captures and viewers."},{"u":"cli.html#agentos-command-reference","p":"CLI","h":"agentos command reference","x":"agentos project is the administrator CLI for project lifecycle. It runs as root. Project agents cannot run it. The package installs it to /usr/sbin/agentos. agentos system is the client for hostd. The administrator and the system agent can use it. The command and service names are provisional."},{"u":"cli.html#commands","p":"CLI","h":"Commands","x":"agentos project create NAME [flags] Creates the identity, directories, and slice. Prints NAME. agentos project list Prints name, UID, running command count, and creation time. agentos project inspect NAME Prints metadata, disk use, command count, and limits as key=value lines. agentos project run NAME -- COMMAND [ARG...] Runs a command in the sandbox and waits. Returns its exit status. agentos project run --detach NAME -- COMMAND [ARG...] Starts a command in the background and prints its 8-character ID. agentos project shell NAME Opens an interactive login shell in the sandbox. agentos project gui NAME SUBCOMMAND ... Runs an agentos-gui subcommand as the project. GUI.md lists them. agentos project ps NAME Prints the IDs of running commands. agentos project logs NAME ID Prints the journal output of a command. agentos project stop NAME [ID] Stops one command, or every command in the project. agentos project toolchain NAME TOOL VERSION Puts an installed global toolchain on the project's PATH. agentos project toolchain NAME TOOL --clear Removes that selection. agentos project snapshot NAME [--label LABEL] [--auto] Takes a read-only snapshot and prints its ID. --auto makes it subject to retention. agentos project snapshots NAME Prints ID, kind, creation time, omitted subvolume count, and label. agentos project snapshot-delete NAME ID --yes Removes one snapshot. agentos project prune NAME [--keep N] Removes the oldest automatic snapshots, beyond the newest N. agentos project restore NAME --snapshot ID --yes Stops all commands, replaces the project's data, and prints the ID of the automatic pre-restore snapshot. agentos project backup NAME --snapshot ID Writes a backup stream to standard output. agentos project recover Recreates a project from a backup stream on standard input. Prints NAME. agentos project reattach Rebuilds each registered project's account and limits from the data partition. Only the installer's repair needs it. agentos project delete NAME --yes Stops all commands and removes all project data, its local snapshots, and the identity. agentos storage check Runs the storage check at once. agentos-storage.timer runs it every minute."},{"u":"cli.html#create-flags","p":"CLI","h":"create flags","x":"--cpu PERCENT None CPUQuota for the whole project. 100 is one core. --memory-high SIZE 70% MemoryHigh for the whole project. --memory-max SIZE 80% MemoryMax for the whole project. --tasks N 4096 TasksMax for the whole project. --disk SIZE 80% Limit on all of the project's subvolumes together. A percentage means a share of the filesystem. SIZE is a number with an optional K, M, G, or T suffix, or a percentage. Memory percentages mean a share of RAM. All projects together are also limited by agentos-project.slice. NAME matches ^a-z?$."},{"u":"cli.html#system-commands","p":"CLI","h":"System commands","x":"Replies are JSON on standard output. Refusals go to standard error. agentos system status Reports host package state, changes by state, and toolchains. agentos system package install NAME... --reason TEXT Plans an install. Applies it at once if policy allows. agentos system package remove NAME... --reason TEXT Plans a removal. agentos system package upgrade --reason TEXT Plans an upgrade of every installed package. It always needs approval. agentos system toolchain install NAME VERSION --reason TEXT Plans a global toolchain install from the catalog. agentos system toolchain remove NAME VERSION --reason TEXT Plans a global toolchain removal. agentos system change show ID Prints one change. agentos system change approve ID Approves a change. Administrator only. agentos system change reject ID Rejects a change. Administrator only. agentos system change apply ID Applies an approved change and verifies it. agentos system change history Prints the last 200 audit events. agentos system recover Finishes any interrupted change. 0 The request succeeded. 1 hostd refused the request, another change is running (busy), the socket is unreachable, or the change failed. 2 Invalid usage. 3 The change is waiting for an administrator."},{"u":"cli.html#project-exit-status","p":"CLI","h":"Project exit status","x":"Status of COMMAND run completed the command. 1 The caller is not root. 2 Invalid usage, unknown project or snapshot, an existing project or account with that name, a refused backup, too little free disk space for a snapshot, or a process that outlives delete or restore. 127 COMMAND is not on the project's PATH."},{"u":"cli.html#command-lifetime","p":"CLI","h":"Command lifetime","x":"SSH connection lost Kept Keeps running Lifetime not guaranteed. Check ps. stop Kept Stopped Stopped Host reboot Kept Not resumed Not resumed restore Replaced Stopped Stopped delete Removed Stopped Stopped Use --detach for any work that must survive a lost connection. If delete fails, run it again. The name stays reserved and the project accepts no commands until a delete completes. If restore fails, run it again with the same snapshot, or run delete. The project accepts no commands until one of them completes. If recover fails, run it again with the same backup. Commands in one project share a network namespace and reach each other on 127.0.0.1. The administrator can reach a project port at the address that inspect prints. A graphical session that agentos project gui NAME start created lasts until stop, agentos project stop, or a reboot. A session that a project command started with agentos-gui start ends with that command. A project's commands start with HOME set to home/ and the working directory set to workspace/. The PATH starts with home/.local/bin, followed by any selected toolchains. The administrator's environment is not passed in."},{"u":"cli.html#package","p":"CLI","h":"Package","x":"packaging/build writes .build/package/agentos_VERSION_amd64.deb. Install it with apt-get install ./agentos_VERSION_amd64.deb. /var/lib/agent-os must be on Btrfs. On any other filesystem the install stops before it unpacks a file."},{"u":"cli.html#vm-image","p":"CLI","h":"VM image","x":"packaging/build-image writes .build/image/agentos_VERSION_amd64.qcow2, with a checksum and a package list beside it. It needs a built test VM, so run tests/vm/vm build first. The image is a minimal Debian 13 with the package already installed. It boots with BIOS firmware and uses DHCP on the first Ethernet interface. It has 8 GiB for the root and a Btrfs data partition that grows to fill the disk on every boot, so enlarge the disk with qemu-img resize before you start it. The image holds no password and no SSH key. Give the first boot the administrator's public key as a systemd credential, or nobody can log in: qemu-system-x86_64 -machine q35,accel=kvm -m 4G -drive file=agentos.qcow2,if=virtio \\ -nic user,model=virtio-net-pci,hostfwd=tcp:127.0.0.1:2222-:22 \\ -smbios \"type=11,value=io.systemd.credential.binary:ssh.authorized_keys.admin=$(base64 -w0 key.pub)\" Log in as admin, which has sudo without a password. Root cannot log in over SSH, and the server accepts only keys. Each machine generates its own host keys and machine ID on first boot. APT points at the live Debian archive."},{"u":"cli.html#installer-iso","p":"CLI","h":"Installer ISO","x":"packaging/build-iso writes .build/installer/agentos_VERSION_amd64.iso, with a checksum and a package list beside it. It needs a built test VM, so run tests/vm/vm build first. Write the ISO to a USB stick, or attach it as a CD. It starts under BIOS or UEFI, and Secure Boot must be off to start it. It installs from the medium and uses no network. Each console shows the installer, so press Enter on the one you are using. The installer asks for these things: The administrator's SSH public key. Paste it, or press Enter to read agentos-admin.pub from the first USB stick that holds one. The disk to install on. It lists writable disks of at least 16 GiB and leaves out the install medium. If the disk already holds Agent OS, whether to repair it or erase it. The disk's name again. An install erases the whole disk, including any earlier Agent OS and its projects. The disk gets a 256 MiB EFI partition, 8 GiB for the root, and a Btrfs data partition with the rest. It boots under BIOS and UEFI, and it does not depend on firmware boot entries. The network comes up with DHCP on the first Ethernet interface. The report shows the machine's host key fingerprint. Compare it on the first connection. Log in as admin with the enrolled key, as with the VM image. If the install stops for any reason, boot the medium and run it again. A disk that did not finish has no boot code."},{"u":"cli.html#repair-a-broken-host","p":"CLI","h":"Repair a broken host","x":"A repair replaces the root and the boot loader and keeps the data partition, so every project, snapshot, and audit record stays. Boot the same medium, give the administrator's key again, choose the disk, and type repair. The new root gets new host keys, the administrator key you entered, and the ISO's Agent OS version. Each project keeps its UID, disk limit, and memory and task limits. A repair stops before it changes anything if the data partition cannot be read. A repair keeps nothing else from the old root. Install each selected toolchain again before a project needs it. The broker policy and storage.conf return to their defaults."},{"u":"cli.html#package-lifecycle","p":"CLI","h":"Package lifecycle","x":"Install Creates agentos-system and the directories under /var/lib/agent-os and /opt/agent-os, enables Btrfs quotas, copies the default policy and storage.conf if they are absent, and starts the firewall, the two hostd sockets, and the storage timer. Upgrade Keeps running projects, their network, and any existing policy or storage.conf. A unit that the administrator disabled stays disabled. The firewall reloads in one transaction, and hostd runs the new binary on its next connection. Interrupted install or upgrade dpkg --configure -a finishes the work. Remove Refused while any project exists. Otherwise stops the services, closes the sockets, and removes the firewall policy. Project data, snapshots, toolchains, /etc/agent-os, and agentos-system stay. Purge Also deletes /etc/agent-os. Everything else that Remove kept stays. A refused dpkg -r leaves the package installed, with the status ri. The next install of the package clears it."},{"u":"administration.html#how-to-install-and-maintain-an-agent-os-host","p":"Administration","h":"How to install and maintain an Agent OS host","x":"This guide is for the administrator, the human with root on a host. It lists the steps for each task and links to the reference pages for details."},{"u":"administration.html#install-a-host","p":"Administration","h":"Install a host","x":"Choose one way to install: A physical machine, or a VM that boots from a CD or USB stick The installer ISO A hypervisor that boots a qcow2 disk The VM image A running Debian 13 with /var/lib/agent-os on Btrfs The package To install from the ISO: Put your SSH public key in a file named agentos-admin.pub on a second USB stick, or keep it ready to paste. The installer does not look for the key on its own medium. Run tests/vm/vm build and then packaging/build-iso on a build machine, and write the ISO from .build/installer/ to a USB stick. Turn off Secure Boot, and boot the machine from the stick. Press Enter on the console you use, and answer the installer's prompts. It asks for the key and the disk, and then for the disk's name again to confirm. Copy the host key fingerprint from the installer's report. When the installer asks, remove the stick and press Enter to restart. The install erases the whole disk."},{"u":"administration.html#log-in-for-the-first-time","p":"Administration","h":"Log in for the first time","x":"Connect as admin with the enrolled key: bash ssh admin@HOST Compare the fingerprint that SSH shows with the one from the installer's report. If they differ, disconnect and reinstall. Run the first checks: bash systemctl --failed sudo agentos system status systemctl --failed lists no unit on a healthy host. In the status reply, dpkg_consistent is true."},{"u":"administration.html#create-a-project-and-start-an-agent","p":"Administration","h":"Create a project and start an agent","x":"Create the project: bash sudo agentos project create my-app Install the harness inside the project. The project has network access and needs no root: bash sudo agentos project shell my-app Start the harness with --detach, so that it keeps running after your SSH connection ends: bash sudo agentos project run --detach my-app -- COMMAND Read its output with the ID that the command printed: bash sudo agentos project logs my-app ID A reboot does not resume commands. Start them again after each reboot. Command lifetime lists what each event does. To limit a project, pass --cpu, --memory-max, --tasks, or --disk to create. Agent OS has no command that changes the limits of an existing project."},{"u":"administration.html#approve-a-system-agents-change","p":"Administration","h":"Approve a system agent's change","x":"A system agent runs under the agentos-system account. Start a harness there with sudo -iu agentos-system. Its host changes go through hostd, and most of them wait for you. Read the plan: bash sudo agentos system change show ID Check the reason and the impact. Reject the change if you do not expect it: bash sudo agentos system change reject ID To accept the change, approve and apply it: bash sudo agentos system change approve ID sudo agentos system change apply ID If apply reports stale, the packages changed after the plan. Request a new plan. agentos system change history prints the audit trail. To let a package or toolchain install without approval, add it to /etc/agent-os/hostd-policy.json. Policy lists the conditions."},{"u":"administration.html#upgrade-the-host","p":"Administration","h":"Upgrade the host","x":"Plan the upgrade. The command exits with status 3 because the upgrade waits for approval: bash sudo agentos system package upgrade --reason \"monthly upgrade\" Approve and apply the change with the ID that the plan printed. Run sudo agentos system status. If reboot_required is true, stop your project commands, reboot the host, and start the commands again. hostd never reboots the host. Running projects keep running through an upgrade. A package that apt holds back appears in impact.kept_back. Agent OS has no published APT repository, so a Debian upgrade does not bring a new agentos version. To upgrade Agent OS itself, copy a newer .deb to the host and install it: sudo apt-get install ./agentos_VERSION_amd64.deb The upgrade keeps hostd-policy.json and storage.conf. New defaults in those files reach only fresh installs."},{"u":"administration.html#finish-an-interrupted-operation","p":"Administration","h":"Finish an interrupted operation","x":"A hostd change sudo agentos system recover A direct install or upgrade of the package sudo dpkg --configure -a agentos project delete Run the same command again. agentos project restore Run the same command again, with the same snapshot. agentos project recover Run the same command again, with the same backup. A fresh install from the installer Boot the medium, and run the installer again. A repair from the installer Boot the medium, and type repair again. Pressing Enter at that prompt erases the whole disk. If status shows a failed change and dpkg_consistent is false, repair dpkg by hand."},{"u":"administration.html#undo-damage-in-a-project","p":"Administration","h":"Undo damage in a project","x":"Find a snapshot from before the damage: bash sudo agentos project snapshots my-app Restore it. The restore stops all commands in the project and prints the ID of a new pre-restore snapshot, which undoes the restore: bash sudo agentos project restore my-app --snapshot ID --yes Only restore takes a snapshot on its own. Run agentos project snapshot NAME before risky work, or add --auto for one that retention can remove. The cache and tmp directories are empty after a restore."},{"u":"administration.html#back-up-a-project","p":"Administration","h":"Back up a project","x":"Run the backup from another machine, so that a compromised host cannot reach older backups. Agent OS does not encrypt or schedule backups. Take a snapshot, and note its ID: bash ssh admin@HOST 'sudo agentos project snapshot my-app --label backup' Pull the backup, and encrypt it on the other machine: bash set -o pipefail ssh admin@HOST 'sudo agentos project backup my-app --snapshot ID' | age -r RECIPIENT > my-app.backup.age.tmp \\ && mv my-app.backup.age.tmp my-app.backup.age Without pipefail, a dropped connection or a failed backup still leaves a file that looks complete. The rename happens only after the whole pipeline succeeds. To restore on a new host, decrypt the backup and pipe it to sudo agentos project recover. The host needs free space for one extra copy of the backup. If the project selected a toolchain, install that toolchain again."},{"u":"administration.html#repair-a-broken-host","p":"Administration","h":"Repair a broken host","x":"A repair replaces the root and the boot loader, and keeps the data partition. Every project, snapshot, and audit record stays. Before you need a repair, keep copies of /etc/agent-os/hostd-policy.json and /etc/agent-os/storage.conf off the host, because the repair resets both. Boot the install medium, give the administrator's key, and choose the disk. Type repair at the prompt, and then the disk's name to confirm. Pressing Enter at the repair prompt erases the whole disk. Log in as admin. The host has new host keys, so remove its old entry from your known_hosts, and compare the new fingerprint with the report. Copy back your hostd-policy.json and storage.conf. Install each toolchain again that a project selected: bash sudo agentos system toolchain install NAME VERSION --reason \"after repair\" Approve and apply each toolchain change, then start your project commands. The repaired host has the ISO's Agent OS version. If that version is older than the one you ran, upgrade Agent OS again."},{"u":"administration.html#respond-to-low-disk-space","p":"Administration","h":"Respond to low disk space","x":"Agent OS acts on its own when free space runs low. Disk pressure lists the thresholds. Below 10% free, each project keeps one automatic snapshot. If free space is still below 5%, the commands of the largest project stop. The check repeats every minute, so a command you start in that project stops again until space is free. To free space: Run sudo agentos project inspect NAME for each project, and compare disk_used_bytes. It counts what the project references now, so it does not show what deleting a snapshot would free. Remove snapshots you no longer need with sudo agentos project snapshot-delete NAME ID --yes. If you need more, remove each project's last automatic snapshot with sudo agentos project prune NAME --keep 0. That snapshot may be the pre-restore one that undoes a restore. Once free space is above 5%, start the stopped commands again."},{"u":"test-plan.html#how-to-run-the-test-procedure","p":"Test plan","h":"How to run the test procedure","x":"This procedure builds the test VM, proves snapshot and reset behavior, runs the isolation and integration suites, and regenerates the baseline."},{"u":"test-plan.html#check-the-prerequisites","p":"Test plan","h":"Check the prerequisites","x":"You need Linux with /dev/kvm, Docker, OpenSSH, and curl. The scripts run QEMU in a pinned Debian container, so you do not install QEMU. Agent OS itself does not depend on Docker."},{"u":"test-plan.html#build-the-vm","p":"Test plan","h":"Build the VM","x":"Run this command from the repository root: tests/vm/vm build The last line is vm: baseline ready. The command downloads the image pinned in tests/vm/image.lock and verifies its SHA-512 sum. It installs packages from the pinned APT snapshot. .vm/baseline is read-only afterward, and .vm/baseline/packages.txt lists every installed package and version. To rebuild from nothing, add --force. Two builds on 2026-11-08 produced identical package lists."},{"u":"test-plan.html#use-the-vm","p":"Test plan","h":"Use the VM","x":"tests/vm/vm up Boots the VM. Does nothing if it is running. tests/vm/vm ssh [COMMAND] Boots if needed and connects as admin, which has passwordless sudo. tests/vm/vm down Shuts down the VM. tests/vm/vm reset Stops the VM and discards all changes since the baseline. tests/vm/vm kill Cuts the VM's power. tests/vm/vm image FILE [--no-key] Discards .vm/run and boots a copy of a built image on a 24 GiB disk. Without --no-key, the first boot enrolls the test key. tests/vm/vm installer ISO [--uefi] [--keep-disk] [--key FILE] Boots the ISO with a blank 24 GiB disk and waits at the installer's first prompt. --uefi uses UEFI firmware and attaches the ISO as a 17 GiB USB stick instead of a CD. --keep-disk keeps the disk from the last run. --key adds a USB stick that holds the file as agentos-admin.pub. Later boots of that disk keep the same firmware. tests/vm/vm boot-code Prints how many non-zero bytes the root disk has in its boot code. It works while the VM runs. tests/vm/vm export-data FILE Writes the stopped VM's data disk to a compressed qcow2 under .vm. packaging/build-image uses it. tests/vm/vm snapshot save NAME Stops the VM and saves both disks. tests/vm/vm snapshot restore NAME Stops the VM and restores both disks. tests/vm/vm snapshot list Lists saved snapshots. SSH listens on 127.0.0.1:10022 only. The VM's key and disks live in .vm/, which Git ignores."},{"u":"test-plan.html#prove-the-vm-tooling","p":"Test plan","h":"Prove the VM tooling","x":"tests/vm/test-lifecycle The last line is PASS: snapshot restore and reset."},{"u":"test-plan.html#run-the-isolation-suite","p":"Test plan","h":"Run the isolation suite","x":"tests/isolation/run The command resets the VM, installs this checkout, creates projects alpha and beta with agentos, and exits non-zero on any failure. The attacker is alpha, and the victim is beta. It writes results.tsv and transcript.log to .vm/results/isolation-TIMESTAMP/. The transcript holds each probe's real error message. Every row of tests/isolation/checks.tsv runs in two modes: plain uses only separate UIDs. It shows what the sandbox adds, and it proves that the probes can succeed. restricted runs each command through agentos project run. A row passes when the attacker's result matches the expected value and the control succeeds. The control runs the same command as the legitimate owner, so a probe with a typo cannot pass as blocked. Exit codes for shell misuse, timeouts, missing executables, and systemd setup failures count as error, and error always fails."},{"u":"test-plan.html#add-a-check","p":"Test plan","h":"Add a check","x":"Add a tab-separated row to tests/isolation/checks.tsv. Use exit 0 for \"the access worked\". Expect gap only for an access that doc/THREAT_MODEL.md lists as accepted."},{"u":"test-plan.html#prove-that-a-sandbox-setting-matters","p":"Test plan","h":"Prove that a sandbox setting matters","x":"To test a setting, delete its line from configs/systemd/native-restricted.properties, run tests/isolation/run, and restore the line. The rows that fail are the ones that depend on the setting."},{"u":"test-plan.html#run-the-integration-suite","p":"Test plan","h":"Run the integration suite","x":"tests/integration/run The last line is PASS: integration. The command needs internet access. It tests these behaviors through the agentos CLI: Project creation, name validation, UID allocation, and command execution. A project-local Node.js and Pi install, with versions pinned in tests/integration/versions.env. A full Pi tool-call loop against stub_model.py, a local server that needs no credentials. CPU, memory, and task limits for one command, for several commands together, and for all projects together. Project network isolation, internet access, and name resolution. A detached command that outlives a killed SSH client. Data, identities, and tools across a reboot. Deletion with running processes, symlinks, read-only subvolumes, a failed first attempt, and concurrent command starts. Recovery of a crashed create, and refusal of accounts that agentos did not allocate."},{"u":"test-plan.html#run-the-hostd-suite","p":"Test plan","h":"Run the hostd suite","x":"tests/hostd/run The last line is PASS: hostd. The command needs internet access. It tests these behaviors: The exit criterion: the system agent requests ripgrep, an administrator approves, and both projects run it. Self-approval, stale plans, republished and tampered archives, failed upgrades, rejected changes, policy limits, and hostile package names. Socket access, forged identity, malformed input, slow clients, full connection slots, and what the system agent cannot do. Two global Node.js versions selected per project, read-only access, bad digests, and failed checks. A broker killed during a package script and during a toolchain install, a concurrent request, and a power cut. packaging/build runs the Rust unit tests and builds the binaries and the agentos package with Debian's toolchain in a pinned container. Every VM deployment runs it, so a failing unit test stops the suites."},{"u":"test-plan.html#run-the-storage-suite","p":"Test plan","h":"Run the storage suite","x":"tests/storage/run The last line is PASS: storage. It tests these behaviors: Subvolume layout, per-project disk limits, and subvolumes that try to escape a limit or forge btrfs output. Snapshot contents, listing, retention, snapshots of a busy project, and interrupted snapshots. Recovery after a destructive edit, undoing a restore, and a restore that fails before and after the swap. Refusing snapshots, pruning, and stopping the largest project under disk pressure. The exit criterion: a backup leaves the VM through standard output, the VM resets, and recover rebuilds the project. Refusal of truncated, corrupted, and crafted backups, with nothing left behind. tests/storage/benchmark prints the measurements that STORAGE.md records."},{"u":"test-plan.html#run-the-gui-suite","p":"Test plan","h":"Run the GUI suite","x":"tests/gui/run The last line is PASS: gui. The command needs internet access, because it installs the GUI packages and Chromium through hostd. It tests these behaviors: The exit criterion: a project agent starts a session, drives a real GTK application with the mouse and keyboard, captures screenshots and video, and stops the session. Chromium inside a session, with its own user namespace sandbox. Two projects with the same session name, Wayland, compositor, viewer, bus, and X11 sockets, and no TCP listener. Remote viewing through SSH, a refused SSH key, and another project's view of the session. Each way that a session ends: stop, agentos project stop, the starting command exiting, a killed supervisor, and delete. tests/gui/benchmark prints the measurements that GUI.md records."},{"u":"test-plan.html#run-the-packaging-suite","p":"Test plan","h":"Run the packaging suite","x":"tests/packaging/run The last line is PASS: packaging. tests/vm/deploy already installs the package for every suite, and this one tests the package itself through dpkg and apt-get: A refused removal while a project exists. An upgrade under a live project, which keeps its process, network, disk limit, administrator edits, and disabled units, and still applies a new firewall policy. A configure killed partway through, then finished by dpkg --configure -a. Remove, reinstall, and purge, including that a UID is not reused and the firewall is gone. A refused install off Btrfs that unpacks nothing, with an install on Btrfs as its control."},{"u":"test-plan.html#run-the-image-suite","p":"Test plan","h":"Run the image suite","x":"tests/image/run The last line is PASS: image. It builds the image, then boots the file that packaging/build-image published, with nothing installed first: A first boot without a key, where nobody can log in. A first boot with a key, on a disk larger than the image. The system has no failed unit, the data filesystem has grown, and only keys and no root login are accepted. Two projects that run, stay apart, reach the internet, and restore a snapshot. A power cut, after which the data, the network, and the login are still there. A second machine from the same image, with its own host key and machine ID."},{"u":"test-plan.html#run-the-installer-suite","p":"Test plan","h":"Run the installer suite","x":"tests/installer/run The last line is PASS: installer. It builds the ISO and answers the installer on the serial console. The VM's serial port accepts input on 127.0.0.1:10023. Only what the installer wrote boots afterward: Under BIOS from a CD, with the key pasted. A value that is not a public key, a private key, and a disk that is not listed are all refused. A refused confirmation that leaves the installed boot code alone. A power cut in the middle of a reinstall, which leaves no boot code, then a run that finishes. The host key fingerprint in the report matches the one the machine serves, and a key that was not enrolled cannot log in. tests/image/guest/first_boot.sh on the installed machine, the same acceptance steps as the image. An upgrade of agentos through hostd, under a running project, then a reboot. Project data, snapshots, toolchains, and the network survive. A destroyed root, a power cut during its repair, and a repair that finishes. Accounts, UIDs, limits, data, snapshots, and the audit log survive, and the toolchain installs again. A repair of an unreadable data partition stops without changing anything. Under UEFI from a USB stick, with the key read from a second stick. The medium is large enough to qualify and still is not offered, and neither stick can be chosen."},{"u":"test-plan.html#run-the-soak-suite","p":"Test plan","h":"Run the soak suite","x":"tests/soak/run [SECONDS] The last line is PASS: soak. SECONDS defaults to 3600 and must be at least 360. Results go to .vm/results/soak-TIMESTAMP/. Three projects, one, two, and three, each run Pi in a detached loop against a local stub model. Each turn writes a file and records its hash in a ledger. A fourth project, noisy, has tight limits. Once a minute, a tick arrives over SSH from outside the VM. It samples the task count and memory of the project slice as the quietest of five readings a second apart, so a harness turn in progress does not count as growth. A tick that fails, or a host that does not answer, fails the run: Each project cannot read another project's file, and the owner can. Each agent and model is still running. Each project takes a snapshot and prunes under load. hostd answers. noisy stays under and then overruns its memory limit and its process limit. Two spinners in noisy share its CPU quota. After the agents finish, the suite checks that: Each project never went more than 60 seconds without finishing a turn, from the start of the run to its end. Every turn had no problem, and the files, read back after dropping caches, match the ledger. Each project kept five automatic snapshots, and the oldest one restores to an earlier ledger. No project task and no broker process remains. Between the first and second half of the run, the median task count grew by at most 20, the median project memory grew by at most 128 MiB, and the median available memory fell by at most 256 MiB. Disk growth stayed under 512 MiB. The agents write about 33 MiB a minute, so this bound can catch broken retention only in a run longer than about 16 minutes. In tests/soak/run 360, only the snapshot count checks retention. Available memory, after dropping caches, is within 256 MiB of the idle baseline. The kernel log has no fault. noisy hit its memory limit on every tick, and no other project lost a process to memory. No unit failed, and delete leaves no project and no account. A run that failed any check, in a tick or after it, keeps its projects for inspection instead. The steady projects are then restored to their oldest snapshot, and their pre-restore snapshot holds the state at the end of the run. The suite does not reboot during the run, because a reboot does not resume commands. tests/integration/run covers reboots, and its limits step covers memory pressure on the whole project slice. One run on 2026-11-09 lasted 3600 seconds in the baseline VM, with 4 vCPUs and 4 GiB of RAM. It passed all 60 ticks and completed 2060 harness turns. Available memory stayed at 3376 MiB or more, and returned to 3561 MiB from an idle baseline of 3580 MiB. Disk use grew by 259 MiB. noisy hit its memory limit 60 times, and no other project lost a process. That run predates the current per-minute, median, and under-limit checks. Its ledgers and samples fit them: the longest gap between turns was 6 seconds, and the medians did not change between the two halves. Two runs on 2026-11-10 lasted 360 seconds each in the same VM, with the per-minute, median, and under-limit checks. Both passed all 6 ticks and completed 237 harness turns, with a longest gap of 6 seconds between turns. Disk use grew by 234 and 240 MiB. The second run used the current five-reading samples, and every task sample read 9. Another run of that length broke each check on purpose: it stalled one agent for 90 seconds, leaked tasks and memory, wrote 600 MiB once, removed noisy's CPU quota, tightened its memory and task limits, and skipped its later memory overruns. The turn-gap, task, project memory, available memory, disk, under-limit, CPU, and per-tick memory checks each went red. A last run leaked only tasks and memory against the five-reading samples. Its task, project memory, and available memory checks went red, and it kept its projects for inspection. A run whose ticks failed, with every check after them passing, also kept its projects. A third clean run of 360 seconds, with the final scripts, passed all 6 ticks, and delete left no project and no account."},{"u":"test-plan.html#run-the-suites-in-github-actions","p":"Test plan","h":"Run the suites in GitHub Actions","x":".github/workflows/tests.yml runs only when you start it, because each run uses about 60 minutes of the private repository's Actions allowance. To start it, use the Actions tab or run gh workflow run Tests --ref BRANCH. GitHub offers both only after the workflow file is on main. The prepare job fails if the runner has no /dev/kvm. It also runs the Rust unit tests. It builds the VM baseline and the two Docker images once, and stores them in the Actions cache. To rebuild them, change an input of the cache key in the workflow, such as tests/vm/image.lock. Each suite then runs in its own job and its own VM, the same as a local run. One failing suite does not stop the others. Each job uploads .vm/results/ and .vm/serial.log as an artifact named SUITE-diagnostics. A failed job adds the guest journal as journal.log. To reproduce a failure, run the suite's command from the workflow matrix on a machine with /dev/kvm. The integration and hostd suites download from snapshot.debian.org, nodejs.org, and the npm registry while they run. If one of them fails on a download, the log shows the failing URL. Rerun the failed job."},{"u":"test-plan.html#regenerate-the-baseline","p":"Test plan","h":"Regenerate the baseline","x":"tests/vm/baseline The command writes doc/BASELINE.md from a capability probe and three fresh boots. The baseline does not yet cover these items from section 8.3 of the architecture, because the components do not exist: harness startup, repository indexing, concurrent builds, global package promotion timing, behavior under pressure, and recovery timing. GUI startup and capture are in GUI.md."},{"u":"baseline.html#debian-baseline-reference","p":"Baseline","h":"Debian baseline reference","x":"tests/vm/baseline generates this file. Do not edit it by hand. The values describe the unmodified test VM. Later phases compare against them. Image: debian-13-generic-amd64-20261103-2651.qcow2 APT snapshot: 20261103T000000Z VM: 4 vCPU, 4 GiB RAM, virtio disks, KVM, QEMU 10.0.13 Development machine: Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-10900K CPU @ 3.70GHz, Linux 7.3.1-1-cachyos"},{"u":"baseline.html#measured-capabilities","p":"Baseline","h":"Measured capabilities","x":"debian 13.7 kernel 6.12.115+deb13.1-amd64 systemd 257 (257.13-1~deb13u1) CONFIG_USER_NS y CONFIG_PID_NS y CONFIG_NET_NS y CONFIG_IPC_NS y CONFIG_CGROUPS y CONFIG_CGROUP_BPF y CONFIG_PSI y CONFIG_SECCOMP_FILTER y CONFIG_BTRFS_FS m CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK y CONFIG_SECURITY_APPARMOR y active LSMs lockdown,capability,landlock,yama,apparmor,tomoyo,bpf,ipe,ima,evm cgroup filesystem cgroup2fs cgroup controllers cpuset cpu io memory hugetlb pids rdma misc PSI cpu readable yes PSI memory readable yes PSI io readable yes sysctl kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone 1 sysctl user.max_user_namespaces 15512 sysctl kernel.yama.ptrace_scope 0 sysctl kernel.dmesg_restrict 1 sysctl kernel.perf_event_paranoid 3 sysctl kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled 2 sysctl fs.protected_hardlinks 1 sysctl fs.protected_symlinks 1 sysctl fs.protected_regular 2 sysctl fs.protected_fifos 1 bubblewrap candidate 0.12.0-1~deb13u1 btrfs snapshot keeps parent files yes btrfs snapshot includes nested subvolume files no btrfs qgroup limit enforced yes btrfs zstd compression mount yes"},{"u":"baseline.html#benchmarks-over-3-fresh-boots","p":"Baseline","h":"Benchmarks over 3 fresh boots","x":"Treat disk and CPU numbers as relative. They depend on the development machine. Boot, kernel (s) 2.306 2.298 2.359 Boot, userspace (s) 1.751 1.726 1.797 Idle CPU, all vCPUs (%) 0.00 0.00 0.01 Idle memory used (MiB) 303 298 314 Processes at idle 148 145 149 Root disk used (MiB) 797 797 797 No-op unit, plain (ms) 13 12 13 No-op command, agentos project run (ms) 59 57 62 SHA-256, one core (MiB/s) 600 599 604 Direct write, ext4 root (MiB/s) 462 436 463 Direct write, Btrfs data (MiB/s) 114 71 122"}];
